visit meals.richrolls.com.

00:00:24.604 --> 00:00:27.913
Do it now, okay, let's do the show.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.040
I moved to America when I was 25

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.190
and I just left everything behind

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.870
and I came with two suitcases

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.478
and I barely spoke the language,

00:00:39.478 --> 00:00:42.995
but it is such a liberating feeling

00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:47.995
to know that everything you need is inside of you.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.740
You don't need anything else.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.040
And I mean, only COVID could have given me

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.040
this experience again so late in life at 44 years old,

00:00:59.082 --> 00:01:02.540
where I was literally sitting in my apartment

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.650
a year ago not knowing if I'm gonna have a restaurant,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.160
not knowing how to pay for the bills for the business

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.160
and coming to terms with the idea,

00:01:16.423 --> 00:01:20.053
I might lose it all and it's okay.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.579
And when you get to that place,

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:26.210
and I hope I don't need to get physically to that place

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:30.100
again, but I hope mentally I can get myself

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.940
into that space for many, many years to come

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.283
because in that space you're very free

00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:41.283
and you're able to make pretty bold moves without fear.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.596
That's Daniel Humm, and this is the Rich Roll Podcast.

00:01:46.596 --> 00:01:49.013
(soft music)

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.120
The Rich Roll Podcast.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.850
Okay, what happens when one of,

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.200
if not the greatest restaurants in the world

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.200
suddenly seemingly out of the blue goes 100% plant-based.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.080
Well for perspective in context

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.881
of the 132 three star Michelin restaurants around the world,

00:02:18.881 --> 00:02:22.410
not a single one is vegan.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.580
So with that, most would say such a move

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.119
is tantamount to financial suicide,

00:02:28.119 --> 00:02:30.769
but Daniel Humm, the world renowned chef

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:35.210
and owner of 11 Madison Park sees it differently.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.452
He sees it as purpose-driven, mission-based

00:02:39.452 --> 00:02:43.610
and the greatest creative challenge of his lifetime,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.110
a native of Switzerland, a former pro cyclist,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.010
and a 251 marathoner Daniel started cooking at 14

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.680
preparing exquisite meals

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.910
and some of the finest Swiss hotels and restaurants

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.640
before earning his first Michelin star at the age of 24,

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.640
in 2003, he moved to the US to become the executive chef

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.202
at Campton place in San Francisco,

00:03:04.202 --> 00:03:06.480
where he received four stars

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.390
from the San Francisco Chronicle.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.658
Three years later, recruited by Danny Meyer.

00:03:10.658 --> 00:03:13.130
Daniel moves to New York

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.573
and becomes the executive chef at 11 Madison Park.

00:03:15.573 --> 00:03:19.628
And he revitalizes this restaurant so completely

00:03:19.628 --> 00:03:24.628
that in 2017, it was named number one on the coveted list

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.320
of the world's 50 best restaurants.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.884
That's all amazing of course,

00:03:28.884 --> 00:03:31.800
but the story really gets interesting when the pandemic hits

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.610
like most restaurants, 11 Madison Park closed its stores.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.372
They did that for almost a year and teetered on bankruptcy,

00:03:39.372 --> 00:03:41.532
but it was during this time that Daniel,

00:03:41.532 --> 00:03:45.010
like many of us started thinking more deeply

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.384
about things like purpose, what he stands for

00:03:48.384 --> 00:03:51.990
and how he could leverage this talent of his

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.790
and the resources at his disposal

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.100
to more meaningfully participate in solutions

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.340
to things like food insecurity,

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.004
the ills of animal agriculture,

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:06.100
and more broadly the inherent unsustainable nature

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.560
of food systems.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.144
In a moment of clarity,

00:04:09.144 --> 00:04:12.620
he decides to make this crazy bold change

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.360
first by converting his kitchen into a commissary

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.960
and kitting out a food truck to provide free meals

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.464
to food insecure New Yorkers,.

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:22.130
He partners with this organization,

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.539
this nonprofit called Rethink Food,

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:27.413
which is committed to creating sustainable and equitable

00:04:27.413 --> 00:04:29.844
food system solutions at scale.

00:04:29.844 --> 00:04:34.123
And finally, upon EMP's recent reopening

00:04:34.123 --> 00:04:39.123
turns the most revered restaurant in the world plant-based

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.901
a restaurant where every meal enjoyed,

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:44.608
pays for five meals freely provided to those in need.

00:04:44.608 --> 00:04:49.608
And that decision when announced was like this bombshell

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.613
sending the food world into a frenzy,

00:04:52.613 --> 00:04:55.460
but Daniel's bet is paying off

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:57.270
within hours of the announcement.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.280
11 Madison Park booked a full month of reservations.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:01.843
And today just five weeks later,

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:06.843
the waitlist exceeds 15,000 people.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.450
Brevity is not the sole of my wit today

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:11.520
I've got much more to say

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.366
about the amazing conversation to come,

00:05:15.683 --> 00:05:18.645
Okay, Danielle, so this is what went down

00:05:18.645 --> 00:05:22.333
the day that 11 Madison Park announced its new menu,

00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:25.413
which was covered extensively in the media.

00:05:25.413 --> 00:05:28.503
I knew I immediately wanted to get Daniel on the show

00:05:28.503 --> 00:05:31.303
as soon as possible because my news feed blowing up

00:05:31.303 --> 00:05:34.270
with people commenting about this incredible story.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:37.233
And my friend past podcast, guests,

00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:39.693
former food and drinks editor for Esquire,

00:05:39.693 --> 00:05:43.343
Jeff Gordoneer who had profiled Daniel for Esquire

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:45.503
on the New York Times, connected us.

00:05:45.503 --> 00:05:47.092
Daniel agreed to do the show,

00:05:47.092 --> 00:05:50.013
but he made that agreement on one condition

00:05:50.013 --> 00:05:53.683
that I first dine at 11 Madison Park.

00:05:53.683 --> 00:05:55.323
And I'm not one to say no to that.

00:05:55.323 --> 00:05:57.537
So I immediately booked my flight to New York City.

00:05:57.537 --> 00:05:59.674
I joined Jeff for said dinner,

00:05:59.674 --> 00:06:03.853
which was, I don't even know how to describe it.

00:06:03.853 --> 00:06:06.293
It was exquisite, it was divine,

00:06:06.293 --> 00:06:08.513
it was like this performance art,

00:06:08.513 --> 00:06:12.316
unlike anything I had previously experienced

00:06:12.316 --> 00:06:14.917
admittedly rare privilege.

00:06:14.917 --> 00:06:16.953
And the following day, I sat down with Daniel

00:06:16.953 --> 00:06:19.223
for the conversation you're about to enjoy.

00:06:19.223 --> 00:06:22.133
It's a conversation about why food at this level

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:27.133
food as curated experience food is art is important.

00:06:27.743 --> 00:06:30.783
It's about what makes a great chef,

00:06:30.783 --> 00:06:33.443
a great restaurant great in the first place.

00:06:33.443 --> 00:06:38.443
And it's about what it really means to pursue a passion.

00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:40.713
It's also about constant reinvention,

00:06:40.713 --> 00:06:41.965
but more than anything,

00:06:41.965 --> 00:06:45.613
this is a conversation about personal evolution,

00:06:45.613 --> 00:06:49.341
the search for meaning and purpose beyond accolades

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:52.393
and what it means to mature your career

00:06:52.393 --> 00:06:55.613
and devote your talents into something mission-based

00:06:55.613 --> 00:06:59.013
in service to a better world.

00:06:59.013 --> 00:07:00.263
Daniel is remarkable.

00:07:00.263 --> 00:07:02.423
His story is wildly fascinating.

00:07:02.423 --> 00:07:05.323
And I don't feel it's an overstatement to say that

00:07:05.323 --> 00:07:07.943
he is now one of the most important figures

00:07:07.943 --> 00:07:09.301
in the plant-based movement.

00:07:09.301 --> 00:07:13.713
I believe 11 Madison Park because of its stature

00:07:13.713 --> 00:07:15.903
because of what this move represents.

00:07:15.903 --> 00:07:19.343
And because of what Daniel imagined and is now creating

00:07:19.343 --> 00:07:23.123
is well-poised to forever change the restaurant industry.

00:07:23.123 --> 00:07:26.693
And also how people think about what plant-based

00:07:26.693 --> 00:07:29.353
cuisine is and can be.

00:07:29.353 --> 00:07:31.573
It was an honor to experience his talents.

00:07:31.573 --> 00:07:33.093
It was an honor to spend time with him.

00:07:33.093 --> 00:07:36.153
And it's an honor to share our exchange with you.

00:07:36.153 --> 00:07:38.820
So without further ado, this is me

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.820
and one of not the best chefs in the world, Daniel Humms.

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:48.743
All right, we're good to do this.

00:07:48.743 --> 00:07:50.178
How are you doing thanks for doing this today.

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:53.122
I'm good, so happy to be here.

00:07:53.122 --> 00:07:54.754
That's very, very sweet of you to say,

00:07:54.754 --> 00:07:59.113
I am thrilled to be talking to you today.

00:07:59.113 --> 00:08:00.500
Thank you for making the time

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.773
and thank you for making room for me at the restaurant

00:08:03.773 --> 00:08:07.373
last night, Jeff Gordoneer you and I had an exquisite meal

00:08:07.373 --> 00:08:09.763
curated by you and your wonderful staff.

00:08:09.763 --> 00:08:13.643
It's a memory that I won't soon forget.

00:08:13.643 --> 00:08:16.343
And it was really quite something.

00:08:16.343 --> 00:08:20.526
I have so many takeaways from the experience,

00:08:20.526 --> 00:08:25.053
not the least of which is coming into this realization

00:08:25.053 --> 00:08:30.053
that you, as the chef and like the team leader,

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.832
of course the centerpiece of this experience is the food,

00:08:33.832 --> 00:08:35.603
but there's so much more

00:08:35.603 --> 00:08:38.273
that goes into curating this experience.

00:08:38.273 --> 00:08:40.503
You have to be this gregarious host.

00:08:40.503 --> 00:08:43.725
You have to be present with all of the patrons,

00:08:43.725 --> 00:08:46.793
despite whatever chaos is going on in the kitchen.

00:08:46.793 --> 00:08:50.913
And I noticed an extraordinary equanimity

00:08:50.913 --> 00:08:53.443
in your disposition as you greeted everybody

00:08:53.443 --> 00:08:57.733
and spent time with each of the patrons that had,

00:08:57.733 --> 00:09:00.329
you know, I'm sure it looked forward to this experience

00:09:00.329 --> 00:09:05.303
and it really belies the trope of the screaming chef

00:09:05.303 --> 00:09:07.913
and the chaos and anarchy

00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:12.913
that we've come to sort of believe to be the rule,

00:09:15.013 --> 00:09:16.613
as opposed to the exception.

00:09:16.613 --> 00:09:18.243
I know you had your moments in the past

00:09:18.243 --> 00:09:19.393
with that kind of behavior,

00:09:19.393 --> 00:09:22.088
but I didn't see any indication of that last night.

00:09:22.088 --> 00:09:27.088
No, I think when you lose your cool as a chef,

00:09:27.363 --> 00:09:29.268
it is a weakness.

00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:33.223
And I mean, we've all been in this situation,

00:09:33.223 --> 00:09:36.639
we as chefs have all been in these situations

00:09:36.639 --> 00:09:40.593
where we lose our cool or were in an environment

00:09:40.593 --> 00:09:42.693
where other people are losing their cool,

00:09:42.693 --> 00:09:47.203
but it is really a weakness of the organization

00:09:47.203 --> 00:09:48.289
of the restaurant.

00:09:48.289 --> 00:09:53.289
And today I think kitchens are far from that

00:09:55.483 --> 00:09:56.743
and especially ours.

00:09:56.743 --> 00:09:59.953
I mean, it's pretty well oiled machine

00:09:59.953 --> 00:10:02.233
and we have so many talented people,

00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:05.553
so there's really no need for that kind of behavior.

00:10:05.553 --> 00:10:08.563
Right, if you have your management structure sorted out

00:10:08.563 --> 00:10:10.758
and you are being an effective leader,

00:10:10.758 --> 00:10:13.683
from kind of a military perspective,

00:10:13.683 --> 00:10:14.997
then there shouldn't be those problems.

00:10:14.997 --> 00:10:17.062
If you're resorting to that kind of behavior,

00:10:17.062 --> 00:10:20.932
it means there's something broken in that chain of command.

00:10:20.932 --> 00:10:22.673
That's exactly right.

00:10:22.673 --> 00:10:26.227
That's exactly, and in fact, it's a shame that

00:10:26.227 --> 00:10:31.227
there are TV shows portraying that kind of behavior

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:36.003
because it's really not how it is, it's not.

00:10:36.003 --> 00:10:37.023
Yeah.
No.

00:10:37.023 --> 00:10:37.856
You've had...

00:10:37.856 --> 00:10:40.783
You used to do a little bit of that though back in the day.

00:10:40.783 --> 00:10:43.881
I mean, what is really difficult

00:10:43.881 --> 00:10:48.881
is when you're a young chef to get to the top

00:10:50.383 --> 00:10:54.143
or to be recognized you have to do it

00:10:54.143 --> 00:10:57.413
with a lot less resources

00:10:57.413 --> 00:11:01.303
than with the people you're competing with.

00:11:01.303 --> 00:11:02.928
Like today we have, you know, we...

00:11:02.928 --> 00:11:06.531
It costs a certain amount to eat at the restaurant.

00:11:06.531 --> 00:11:07.781
Yeah.

00:11:07.781 --> 00:11:11.406
People from all over the world wanna work with me.

00:11:12.463 --> 00:11:15.901
So we have so much going for us,

00:11:15.901 --> 00:11:18.362
but when you're young and you know,

00:11:18.362 --> 00:11:22.723
the meal can't be that expensive, no one knows who you are.

00:11:22.723 --> 00:11:27.523
So you're working with just whoever you find.

00:11:27.523 --> 00:11:32.061
And it's kind of like a smoking mirror kind of situation

00:11:32.061 --> 00:11:34.819
to rise up up the ranks.

00:11:34.819 --> 00:11:39.413
And so it's just a lot of pressure on the individual

00:11:39.413 --> 00:11:41.801
who is trying to make sure.

00:11:41.801 --> 00:11:44.803
I wanna get into the reinvention,

00:11:44.803 --> 00:11:48.293
the re imagination of food, all the exciting developments

00:11:48.293 --> 00:11:50.583
that are taking place at 11 Madison Park.

00:11:50.583 --> 00:11:52.603
But before we do that,

00:11:52.603 --> 00:11:54.653
I think it would be cool to spend a few minutes

00:11:54.653 --> 00:11:58.343
just talking about the importance of cuisine in general.

00:11:58.343 --> 00:11:59.939
And just by way of personal background,

00:11:59.939 --> 00:12:03.103
I'm a pretty simple guy.

00:12:03.103 --> 00:12:06.193
I'm a rice and beans and guacamole dude.

00:12:06.193 --> 00:12:07.713
Like I eat proper food.

00:12:07.713 --> 00:12:09.883
I've been plant-based for a long time.

00:12:09.883 --> 00:12:11.911
My tastes are not that refined.

00:12:11.911 --> 00:12:14.174
I have had privileged experiences,

00:12:14.174 --> 00:12:17.743
a couple of eating at fine establishment,

00:12:17.743 --> 00:12:21.593
yours being certainly one of them I ate at Noma two years

00:12:21.593 --> 00:12:24.163
ago with Jeff I've eaten at Lara Pesh.

00:12:24.163 --> 00:12:27.693
I've had those experiences, but I wouldn't consider myself

00:12:27.693 --> 00:12:31.213
like a gastronomist by any stretch of the imagination.

00:12:31.213 --> 00:12:33.703
So and I think it's important to say like,

00:12:33.703 --> 00:12:38.301
most people in their lives will never have the privilege

00:12:38.301 --> 00:12:40.883
of dining at your restaurant

00:12:40.883 --> 00:12:44.653
or a similar analogous type situation.

00:12:44.653 --> 00:12:49.653
So why, like, why is cuisine at this level important?

00:12:51.683 --> 00:12:55.673
How do you think about food as art at the highest level

00:12:55.673 --> 00:12:57.293
and the impact that has

00:12:57.293 --> 00:13:00.676
and the meaning that carries throughout culture?

00:13:01.693 --> 00:13:04.123
Well, I think it's an art form.

00:13:04.123 --> 00:13:07.641
I've always cooked in those restaurants.

00:13:07.641 --> 00:13:10.135
From the beginning of my career,

00:13:10.135 --> 00:13:12.740
I worked in these Michelin star restaurants.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:16.193
And for me, the way I've I've experienced it

00:13:16.193 --> 00:13:21.193
was as a performance and as an art form.

00:13:21.952 --> 00:13:26.952
And it's very much tied to culture of the place.

00:13:28.117 --> 00:13:31.193
Like when you're working in a restaurant in France

00:13:31.193 --> 00:13:34.779
or in Italy or in South America,

00:13:34.779 --> 00:13:38.093
those experiences are very much different.

00:13:38.093 --> 00:13:41.036
They're very much tied to the place

00:13:41.036 --> 00:13:46.036
and they're making references to the culture.

00:13:46.107 --> 00:13:49.982
And of course coming, I grew up in Switzerland

00:13:49.982 --> 00:13:54.723
and fine dining for us was coming out of France,

00:13:54.723 --> 00:13:58.946
which is maybe even the birthplace of that kind of dining.

00:13:59.825 --> 00:14:03.073
I think there's a beautiful culture tied to it.

00:14:03.073 --> 00:14:07.046
And it starts from the farmers,

00:14:07.943 --> 00:14:12.403
the artisans who make the ceramics and the silverware

00:14:12.403 --> 00:14:13.776
and the glass blowing.

00:14:15.093 --> 00:14:17.453
And then it's the people who make the linens

00:14:17.453 --> 00:14:21.393
and then of course the team that works the floor

00:14:21.393 --> 00:14:24.103
and the way they move through the dining room.

00:14:24.103 --> 00:14:27.443
And then of course the chefs and then the recipes

00:14:27.443 --> 00:14:31.896
they're inspired by that go way back sometimes.

00:14:32.876 --> 00:14:34.823
So I think it's...

00:14:34.823 --> 00:14:39.823
And I struggled with this many times in my life

00:14:42.243 --> 00:14:44.993
with the idea of how much it costs to eat

00:14:44.993 --> 00:14:46.649
at one of those restaurants,

00:14:46.649 --> 00:14:51.623
because food is so magical on so many levels

00:14:51.623 --> 00:14:56.593
and food touches everyone and to cook for an audience

00:14:56.593 --> 00:15:01.593
of the 1% that can be challenging sometimes.

00:15:02.843 --> 00:15:05.933
But through the pandemic,

00:15:05.933 --> 00:15:10.303
somehow I found a whole new meaning in all of it.

00:15:10.303 --> 00:15:15.303
Like our restaurant shut down March 16 of last year.

00:15:15.393 --> 00:15:20.333
And all we have left was an empty space

00:15:20.333 --> 00:15:25.333
farmers with so much food that was going bad

00:15:26.112 --> 00:15:30.323
and food insecurity that was doubling.

00:15:30.323 --> 00:15:33.455
And so we transformed 11 Madison Park

00:15:33.455 --> 00:15:35.553
into a community kitchen.

00:15:35.553 --> 00:15:38.863
Yeah, so you go from in 2017,

00:15:38.863 --> 00:15:42.573
the restaurants rated the number one restaurant in the world

00:15:42.573 --> 00:15:45.276
on that coveted 50 world's best list.

00:15:45.276 --> 00:15:46.883
I'm sure with that,

00:15:46.883 --> 00:15:49.753
there's pressure to continue what you're doing,

00:15:49.753 --> 00:15:50.843
the way that you're doing it.

00:15:50.843 --> 00:15:53.263
It's obviously working and it's attracting people

00:15:53.263 --> 00:15:56.313
from all over the world and you're being alauded for it.

00:15:56.313 --> 00:15:59.390
And you're getting all this attention 2020 hits.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:01.533
You're forced to close the restaurant

00:16:01.533 --> 00:16:03.603
you're facing bankruptcy.

00:16:03.603 --> 00:16:06.553
You make some interesting pivots during the pandemic year,

00:16:06.553 --> 00:16:11.553
but in 2021, you open as a 100% plant-based restaurant.

00:16:12.313 --> 00:16:15.843
None of the, other of the 132 three star restaurants

00:16:15.843 --> 00:16:19.119
are vegan, I mean, that requires immense balls

00:16:19.119 --> 00:16:20.823
to make such a bold move.

00:16:20.823 --> 00:16:24.577
But reinvention is really the touchdown of everything

00:16:24.577 --> 00:16:26.813
that you've done historically throughout

00:16:26.813 --> 00:16:28.823
not just your career but your life,

00:16:28.823 --> 00:16:31.517
like you're constantly pivoting and iterating

00:16:31.517 --> 00:16:35.750
and challenging yourself to do something fresh and new.

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:39.533
I think it's one part for selfish reasons

00:16:39.533 --> 00:16:42.477
to keep myself interested in it,

00:16:42.477 --> 00:16:46.023
because I don't see myself doing the same thing

00:16:46.948 --> 00:16:48.853
over and over again.

00:16:48.853 --> 00:16:52.202
And especially after experiencing the pandemic,

00:16:52.202 --> 00:16:53.840
I definitely didn't wanna go back

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:56.153
to doing what we did before.

00:16:56.153 --> 00:16:59.813
Yeah, it seems like it's a bit of a reckoning.

00:16:59.813 --> 00:17:02.593
I mean, I think in the sort of hoity-toity

00:17:02.593 --> 00:17:05.621
high altitude of cuisine at this level,

00:17:05.621 --> 00:17:08.813
there's a lot of discussion and dialogue

00:17:08.813 --> 00:17:11.533
about things that are important within that subculture,

00:17:11.533 --> 00:17:13.913
but actually don't really matter, you know

00:17:13.913 --> 00:17:16.698
they're nice, they're ego inflating, et cetera.

00:17:16.698 --> 00:17:19.223
But the pandemic, my sense is that

00:17:19.223 --> 00:17:23.233
it really compelled you to kind of confront your path

00:17:23.233 --> 00:17:24.350
in a very existential way.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:29.053
And it provided you this opportunity to re-imagine

00:17:29.053 --> 00:17:31.958
and infuse your talent and everything that you do

00:17:31.958 --> 00:17:36.958
with this extraordinary mission-based level of purpose

00:17:38.013 --> 00:17:40.980
and intention that can help people beyond

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:45.253
the very few who can experience the food at 11 Madison Park.

00:17:45.253 --> 00:17:50.253
Now, for sure, and in 2017, when we became one,

00:17:50.432 --> 00:17:54.203
it was actually not such a great,

00:17:54.203 --> 00:17:57.853
it wasn't as great as one would imagine that feeling

00:17:57.853 --> 00:18:01.783
it was actually coming with a lot of emptiness

00:18:01.783 --> 00:18:03.295
and maybe it has to do that.

00:18:03.295 --> 00:18:07.928
This was a goal that we were chasing for so long

00:18:07.928 --> 00:18:11.843
probably for 10 years that number one spot

00:18:11.843 --> 00:18:14.789
was sort of our carrot that we were chasing

00:18:14.789 --> 00:18:19.789
and you've done marathons and triathlons.

00:18:20.163 --> 00:18:23.613
And sometimes when you train a lot for an event

00:18:23.613 --> 00:18:27.543
and then you have the event and you're on such a high,

00:18:27.543 --> 00:18:30.123
you can kind of fall into a hole a little bit

00:18:30.123 --> 00:18:32.373
where you're a little depressed.

00:18:32.373 --> 00:18:35.191
And I think that's definitely what happened to me

00:18:35.191 --> 00:18:37.630
after becoming number one.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.433
And I was looking for more meaning

00:18:40.433 --> 00:18:43.163
I was looking for a higher purpose

00:18:43.163 --> 00:18:47.923
and then the pandemic hit and sort of,

00:18:47.923 --> 00:18:52.923
I realized that my language is food is cooking.

00:18:53.217 --> 00:18:57.696
And actually food is part of everyone's lives,

00:19:01.084 --> 00:19:05.121
but food is so central to so many problems we're facing

00:19:05.121 --> 00:19:08.433
on this planet food insecurity being one

00:19:08.433 --> 00:19:13.433
global warming and farming and so much more.

00:19:15.353 --> 00:19:18.443
And so I found myself in this spot

00:19:18.443 --> 00:19:21.302
of having this kind of incredible opportunity

00:19:21.302 --> 00:19:24.576
to actually say something through the food,

00:19:24.576 --> 00:19:29.576
to show a delicious way to cooking with only plants

00:19:31.223 --> 00:19:34.963
and feeling that could make a real difference.

00:19:34.963 --> 00:19:37.919
The precursor to this is this decision to open up

00:19:37.919 --> 00:19:41.163
11 Madison Park as a commissary kitchen

00:19:41.163 --> 00:19:45.463
and start providing meals to the food insecure.

00:19:45.463 --> 00:19:48.163
And doing that in partnership with Rethink,

00:19:48.163 --> 00:19:51.369
which was founded by one of your former cooks.

00:19:51.369 --> 00:19:52.363
Yeah
Right

00:19:52.363 --> 00:19:54.466
So walk me through that chapter.

00:19:54.466 --> 00:19:59.466
Yeah, so one of our cooks three years before the pandemic

00:20:00.893 --> 00:20:02.233
who worked with us,

00:20:02.233 --> 00:20:07.233
he said, hey, I see you're having all these leftovers

00:20:07.293 --> 00:20:08.993
or these parts of like.

00:20:08.993 --> 00:20:11.185
You know, we are only using the top part of a broccoli

00:20:11.185 --> 00:20:15.443
and the stem we don't use and that is true

00:20:15.443 --> 00:20:17.263
to so many other ingredients.

00:20:17.263 --> 00:20:20.650
And he said, "What if I would take all these ingredients

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.240
"and if I would have a little commissary kitchen

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:26.680
"and I would make meals out of these ingredients

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.431
"for people in need would you be able to support me?"

00:20:30.431 --> 00:20:34.773
So we founded together this organization called Rethink Food

00:20:35.883 --> 00:20:38.563
that was relying on restaurants,

00:20:38.563 --> 00:20:42.143
supplying them with their leftovers.

00:20:42.143 --> 00:20:47.143
And we did that, and that was, you know, it was successful,

00:20:47.333 --> 00:20:51.358
but it was limited of how many meals we could really do,

00:20:51.358 --> 00:20:53.500
but then the pandemic hit

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.493
and obviously all the restaurants closed.

00:20:56.493 --> 00:21:00.063
And so the supply to our commissary kitchens

00:21:00.063 --> 00:21:02.391
to prepare these meals also stopped.

00:21:02.391 --> 00:21:05.513
And I just found myself in this position

00:21:05.513 --> 00:21:09.973
where I had this organization Rethink

00:21:09.973 --> 00:21:13.200
where I had an empty kitchen, a massive professional,

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:18.200
empty kitchen where I knew a lot of cooks without chops

00:21:19.306 --> 00:21:23.325
and where I knew all the farmers and producers

00:21:23.325 --> 00:21:26.626
sitting on food that they're not selling anymore now.

00:21:27.563 --> 00:21:32.513
And so we realized that we had to change what Rethink was.

00:21:32.513 --> 00:21:35.233
And we sort of started this program

00:21:35.233 --> 00:21:36.792
called Rethink certified.

00:21:36.792 --> 00:21:39.393
And we allowed other restaurants,

00:21:39.393 --> 00:21:41.713
any restaurant to use their kitchen

00:21:41.713 --> 00:21:46.253
and their staff and their farmers to produce these meals.

00:21:46.253 --> 00:21:50.873
And we as Rethink organization are buying these meals

00:21:50.873 --> 00:21:51.833
from the restaurant.

00:21:51.833 --> 00:21:56.813
So actually restaurants were able to keep people employed,

00:21:56.813 --> 00:22:01.813
farmers who are able to keep supplying foods

00:22:01.934 --> 00:22:04.552
and people were getting fed.

00:22:04.552 --> 00:22:07.154
So that was a really pivotal moment,

00:22:07.154 --> 00:22:10.750
changed the Rethink organization,

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:15.750
but also changed my belief of cooking for the 1%.

00:22:16.543 --> 00:22:21.543
Because cooking for the 1% allowed me to have this voice

00:22:22.153 --> 00:22:25.467
to be able to raise the funds we needed

00:22:25.467 --> 00:22:29.273
to be able to get other people on board.

00:22:29.273 --> 00:22:32.533
And so I promised myself

00:22:32.533 --> 00:22:35.593
that if I would reopen 11 Madison Park,

00:22:35.593 --> 00:22:38.026
it would have to have a higher purpose.

00:22:38.026 --> 00:22:43.026
So today with every guest that comes to our restaurant,

00:22:43.119 --> 00:22:45.713
we feed five people forward.

00:22:45.713 --> 00:22:47.486
We actually started a food truck,

00:22:48.389 --> 00:22:52.409
the 11 Madison truck in partnership with the Rethink

00:22:52.409 --> 00:22:57.409
where we have 100 guests tonight, we give 500 meals per day

00:22:59.493 --> 00:23:03.643
away for free in communities of need.

00:23:03.643 --> 00:23:06.383
We cook the food in our own restaurant,

00:23:06.383 --> 00:23:09.483
our staff that also cooks the fancy emails,

00:23:09.483 --> 00:23:13.283
and then our dining room crew that serves our guests

00:23:13.283 --> 00:23:16.207
in the restaurant also go out into these neighborhoods

00:23:16.207 --> 00:23:18.213
and delivering these meals.

00:23:18.213 --> 00:23:20.823
So it's sort of like a circular economy.

00:23:20.823 --> 00:23:21.946
Right, right, right.

00:23:21.946 --> 00:23:26.946
The problem of food insecurity and the problem of food waste

00:23:27.513 --> 00:23:30.404
dovetail, because the solution to one

00:23:30.404 --> 00:23:34.243
to the former can be found in the ladder in so many ways.

00:23:34.243 --> 00:23:37.703
And we are in this extraordinary predicament where,

00:23:37.703 --> 00:23:38.903
more than 50 million people

00:23:38.903 --> 00:23:41.474
lack, reliable access to nutritious meals.

00:23:41.474 --> 00:23:45.843
70 tons of good food goes under utilized every year.

00:23:45.843 --> 00:23:49.393
If you talk to Paul Hawkin who wrote Draw Down,

00:23:49.393 --> 00:23:51.013
he will tell you that food waste

00:23:51.013 --> 00:23:54.425
is one of the biggest contributors to climate change

00:23:54.425 --> 00:23:57.903
solving this problem in your local community is one thing,

00:23:57.903 --> 00:24:02.613
scaling it in order to address the larger national

00:24:02.613 --> 00:24:04.303
and sort of international issues

00:24:04.303 --> 00:24:06.333
that relate to this is another thing.

00:24:06.333 --> 00:24:08.354
But I think what's interesting

00:24:08.354 --> 00:24:11.643
about trying to solve this problem,

00:24:11.643 --> 00:24:13.175
grappling with the solution

00:24:13.175 --> 00:24:17.503
orients itself around communication and distribution.

00:24:17.503 --> 00:24:21.722
It's not food insecurity, isn't by dent of lack of food.

00:24:21.722 --> 00:24:26.722
It's related to ineffectual distribution of the food

00:24:26.763 --> 00:24:28.194
that gets wasted in so many restaurants

00:24:28.194 --> 00:24:30.913
and homes every single day.

00:24:30.913 --> 00:24:34.868
But to go to a busy chef or a busy restaurant

00:24:34.868 --> 00:24:38.713
and layer on top of their impossible schedule,

00:24:38.713 --> 00:24:41.823
the idea that they have to prepare meals or set aside,

00:24:41.823 --> 00:24:43.330
it's like you're creating a situation

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:45.670
that's probably not gonna work.

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:49.753
So what I understand about Rethink is training chefs

00:24:49.753 --> 00:24:53.961
and restaurants and creating systems through technology

00:24:53.961 --> 00:24:56.433
and effective communication tools

00:24:56.433 --> 00:24:58.843
that allow it to be seamless.

00:24:58.843 --> 00:25:01.953
So it doesn't create a burden on these restaurants.

00:25:01.953 --> 00:25:05.283
It just becomes part of how they operate.

00:25:05.283 --> 00:25:06.413
Is that a fair?

00:25:06.413 --> 00:25:09.628
No, I mean, what you're saying is absolutely accurate.

00:25:09.628 --> 00:25:14.443
It's about communication and there is enough food

00:25:14.443 --> 00:25:17.183
for everyone and that's what's so heartbreaking.

00:25:17.183 --> 00:25:19.511
It is actually a problem we can solve.

00:25:19.511 --> 00:25:21.826
There is actually enough food

00:25:21.826 --> 00:25:24.750
and the restaurant business

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:28.907
is not the most lucrative business to be in.

00:25:28.907 --> 00:25:32.363
And actually the goal of Rethink is actually

00:25:32.363 --> 00:25:35.403
that it's a benefit to the restaurant to get on board,

00:25:35.403 --> 00:25:38.147
to be a Rethink certified restaurant.

00:25:38.147 --> 00:25:39.933
Yeah, so talk about that a little bit.

00:25:39.933 --> 00:25:41.673
It's sort of like lead certification for restaurants.

00:25:41.673 --> 00:25:44.289
And I think it plays into this idea

00:25:44.289 --> 00:25:47.584
that especially amongst younger people,

00:25:47.584 --> 00:25:51.503
purpose-driven companies, organizations

00:25:51.503 --> 00:25:54.113
are really crucial in consumers

00:25:54.113 --> 00:25:56.623
making conscious choices about the companies

00:25:56.623 --> 00:25:59.523
and the businesses that they choose to patronize.

00:25:59.523 --> 00:26:03.005
Yeah, for me, really the light bulb went off

00:26:03.005 --> 00:26:05.358
right as the pandemic started.

00:26:05.358 --> 00:26:10.143
And I decided that we would start cooking these meals.

00:26:10.143 --> 00:26:13.945
I went to different soup kitchens around the city

00:26:13.945 --> 00:26:15.983
to learn as much as I could

00:26:15.983 --> 00:26:19.877
how they were operating and you know, how meals are packaged

00:26:19.877 --> 00:26:22.573
and what were the meals consists of

00:26:22.573 --> 00:26:25.112
and what gets good feedback and whatnot.

00:26:25.112 --> 00:26:28.750
And I realized that all these soup kitchens

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:31.072
were run by volunteers.

00:26:31.072 --> 00:26:36.072
And a lot of them weren't as well organized.

00:26:36.503 --> 00:26:38.974
They also didn't have the connection that we have

00:26:38.974 --> 00:26:42.103
to producers and farmers.

00:26:42.103 --> 00:26:45.315
And so when we started producing these meals,

00:26:45.315 --> 00:26:48.253
I was shocked how efficient we were.

00:26:48.253 --> 00:26:52.433
We were able to produce 5,000 meals a day, pretty quickly.

00:26:52.433 --> 00:26:54.036
That's a lot of meals.

00:26:54.036 --> 00:26:56.272
We were efficient in time.

00:26:56.272 --> 00:26:59.056
We were efficient at price too.

00:26:59.923 --> 00:27:00.756
We were set...

00:27:00.756 --> 00:27:04.693
We were told that a meal is probably $5 per meal.

00:27:04.693 --> 00:27:08.653
And within a week, our meals were like around $2.

00:27:08.653 --> 00:27:12.193
So then I realized that every restaurant

00:27:12.193 --> 00:27:14.689
produces waste as well, like you said,

00:27:14.689 --> 00:27:18.823
and I realized that the chefs and the restaurant

00:27:18.823 --> 00:27:20.243
have a responsibility

00:27:20.243 --> 00:27:22.659
and are in the center of this conversation

00:27:22.659 --> 00:27:24.402
because we're talking to farms every day,

00:27:24.402 --> 00:27:28.473
we're producing waste every day, we have talented cooks,

00:27:28.473 --> 00:27:30.011
talented team members.

00:27:30.011 --> 00:27:33.509
So the efficiencies in the restaurant are far greater than

00:27:33.509 --> 00:27:36.263
in commissary kitchens.

00:27:36.263 --> 00:27:40.123
And then the restaurants also struggle often to pay,

00:27:40.123 --> 00:27:43.009
their rent and their staff and so forth.

00:27:43.009 --> 00:27:48.009
And so if the organization is already paying soup kitchens,

00:27:48.318 --> 00:27:50.793
they might as well pay restaurants.

00:27:50.793 --> 00:27:53.313
So there's actually a great benefit.

00:27:53.313 --> 00:27:56.133
Yeah, it gives them a base level of predictability

00:27:56.133 --> 00:27:57.794
in their income that can create almost

00:27:57.794 --> 00:28:01.552
like a universal basic income for restaurants.

00:28:01.552 --> 00:28:03.505
As long as they're providing the service,

00:28:03.505 --> 00:28:06.513
they know they can pay a certain percentage of their bills

00:28:06.513 --> 00:28:08.087
and keep the lights on.
Absolutely.

00:28:08.087 --> 00:28:10.563
How many people show up every day.

00:28:10.563 --> 00:28:12.050
Yeah, that was exactly the idea.

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:13.696
It helps for pay for the rent.

00:28:13.696 --> 00:28:17.914
It says it's a consistent income.

00:28:17.914 --> 00:28:20.383
No one knows better than chefs,

00:28:20.383 --> 00:28:24.913
how to cook delicious inexpensive meals.

00:28:24.913 --> 00:28:27.461
You were sharing last night a little bit about

00:28:27.461 --> 00:28:32.461
the impact of visiting these various neighborhoods

00:28:33.283 --> 00:28:37.425
around New York City and really being boots on the ground

00:28:37.425 --> 00:28:39.012
and developing a greater appreciation

00:28:39.012 --> 00:28:42.343
and understanding for how most people

00:28:42.343 --> 00:28:46.133
struggle every single day to get access to nutritious food.

00:28:46.133 --> 00:28:49.093
So talk a little bit about that experience.

00:28:49.093 --> 00:28:53.253
Yeah, I mean, it's shocking and it's unacceptable

00:28:53.253 --> 00:28:55.369
that in a city like New York City,

00:28:55.369 --> 00:28:56.643
that kind of poverty

00:28:56.643 --> 00:29:01.643
and not accessibility to food is a reality.

00:29:03.118 --> 00:29:07.563
I mean, yesterday I went to visit the Queensbridge

00:29:07.563 --> 00:29:11.656
housing project because I'm really interested in,

00:29:12.503 --> 00:29:16.803
you know, we, me as 11 Madison Park,

00:29:16.803 --> 00:29:19.133
we can't change the entire world,

00:29:19.133 --> 00:29:20.693
but we got to start somewhere.

00:29:20.693 --> 00:29:22.933
So I'm really trying to figure out

00:29:22.933 --> 00:29:25.423
where would it be the most meaningful.

00:29:25.423 --> 00:29:28.943
So I've been visiting different community centers,

00:29:28.943 --> 00:29:30.543
different community leaders.

00:29:30.543 --> 00:29:33.955
I mean, the one thing that is so incredible,

00:29:33.955 --> 00:29:37.983
you know, and there are so many bad neighborhoods

00:29:38.913 --> 00:29:41.502
in New York and all over the country,

00:29:41.502 --> 00:29:46.043
but even as bad as they are, there are always these angels,

00:29:46.043 --> 00:29:49.273
these incredible people who are doing good,

00:29:49.273 --> 00:29:50.923
who are helping others.

00:29:50.923 --> 00:29:52.500
And sometimes you wonder, wow,

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:54.733
where do they take this energy from?

00:29:54.733 --> 00:29:57.773
But it's just so beautiful and that has been consistent

00:29:57.773 --> 00:29:59.297
in wherever I have visited.

00:29:59.297 --> 00:30:02.332
And yesterday in Queensbridge,

00:30:02.332 --> 00:30:07.332
I mean, this is a six block radius,

00:30:07.783 --> 00:30:12.783
you know, square that has 69 apartment buildings

00:30:13.823 --> 00:30:17.917
that houses 12,000 people.

00:30:17.917 --> 00:30:22.917
And there's one little grocery store

00:30:23.203 --> 00:30:27.416
where the fresh food section is one little corner.

00:30:27.416 --> 00:30:29.123
I mean, just unbelievable.

00:30:29.123 --> 00:30:31.219
There is no access to fresh food

00:30:31.219 --> 00:30:34.903
and it's 20 minutes from here.

00:30:34.903 --> 00:30:36.423
We're in downtown Manhattan.

00:30:36.423 --> 00:30:37.753
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:30:37.753 --> 00:30:42.643
So the solution rests and I mean, you can deliver meals,

00:30:42.643 --> 00:30:44.753
you can deliver food to these communities,

00:30:44.753 --> 00:30:48.120
but that's a kin to a band-aid in the sense that

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:49.830
rather than teaching them how to fish,

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:51.633
you're providing a fish, right?

00:30:51.633 --> 00:30:54.500
So the long-term solution rests in

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:58.403
empowering these remarkable people in the communities,

00:30:58.403 --> 00:31:01.390
these catalyst, these lightning rods for change

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:03.023
and providing them with the access

00:31:03.023 --> 00:31:05.953
and the tools that they require

00:31:05.953 --> 00:31:07.753
to empower their own neighborhoods.

