as an economy vehicle, I'm not sure it would have worked.

00:46:01.903 --> 00:46:06.903
His first car was like a $250,000 Roadster,

00:46:07.121 --> 00:46:08.733
and they only produced a few.

00:46:08.733 --> 00:46:12.243
So the idea being, create an aspirational brand,

00:46:12.243 --> 00:46:14.923
you come out with the luxury product first

00:46:14.923 --> 00:46:16.713
over time with scale,

00:46:16.713 --> 00:46:19.361
you can create products that consumers

00:46:19.361 --> 00:46:21.713
can get more accessibly.

00:46:21.713 --> 00:46:23.063
Yeah, I think that's exactly.

00:46:23.063 --> 00:46:25.643
That was my thought a little bit

00:46:25.643 --> 00:46:29.093
that could have a massive, massive, massive impact.

00:46:29.093 --> 00:46:29.926
Yeah.

00:46:29.926 --> 00:46:31.523
Because we are going there actually.

00:46:31.523 --> 00:46:32.403
Right.

00:46:32.403 --> 00:46:33.573
This is--

00:46:33.573 --> 00:46:35.083
So talk about that, I mean,

00:46:35.083 --> 00:46:37.213
we still haven't gotten down to the nitty gritty of like,

00:46:37.213 --> 00:46:39.713
why 100% plant-based, could you have a little fish?

00:46:39.713 --> 00:46:42.303
Why don't, you know, why is there a little bit of me,

00:46:42.303 --> 00:46:45.963
like, why are you so ardently doing this,

00:46:45.963 --> 00:46:47.893
in this rigorous way,

00:46:47.893 --> 00:46:50.163
as somebody who's been plant-based for 15 years, I get it.

00:46:50.163 --> 00:46:52.333
I'm just interested in like your,

00:46:52.333 --> 00:46:54.643
how you think about that for yourself.

00:46:54.643 --> 00:46:57.553
Well, for us it was just exciting.

00:46:57.553 --> 00:47:00.193
One part, it was exciting to challenge ourselves

00:47:00.193 --> 00:47:01.393
to that kind of level.

00:47:01.393 --> 00:47:04.983
Like in the beginning, it seemed so daunting

00:47:04.983 --> 00:47:09.983
to let go of all these ingredients that we worked with

00:47:10.453 --> 00:47:12.703
for so many years and even ingredients

00:47:12.703 --> 00:47:14.823
that made our restaurant famous.

00:47:14.823 --> 00:47:18.860
I mean, we perfected cooking the lobster during the duck.

00:47:18.860 --> 00:47:20.813
And very rich food.

00:47:20.813 --> 00:47:23.166
Yeah, mostly rich food.

00:47:23.166 --> 00:47:28.166
But what I've learned is that by letting go of these things

00:47:30.853 --> 00:47:34.313
today, I feel so liberated in a way

00:47:34.313 --> 00:47:36.373
that I never thought was possible.

00:47:36.373 --> 00:47:40.383
As a chef, I'm more excited than I've ever been.

00:47:40.383 --> 00:47:45.323
And I feel my palette of ingredients has only grown

00:47:45.323 --> 00:47:49.493
because before, in a way, we were stuck with these things,

00:47:49.493 --> 00:47:52.883
you had to have lobster, you had to have the duck,

00:47:52.883 --> 00:47:54.973
you had to have a photograph.

00:47:54.973 --> 00:47:59.973
And today we're rewriting an entire new language.

00:48:01.343 --> 00:48:04.283
It's not clear what the main course is gonna be

00:48:04.283 --> 00:48:08.103
or the appetizer it's like the range of ingredients.

00:48:08.103 --> 00:48:09.583
Yeah, it's creatively inspired.

00:48:09.583 --> 00:48:14.303
I think there is something to this idea of constraints,

00:48:14.303 --> 00:48:17.873
driving innovation in the way that an art,

00:48:17.873 --> 00:48:20.313
if an artist is restricted in their color palette,

00:48:20.313 --> 00:48:25.313
or a movie director is suddenly facing budget cuts

00:48:25.693 --> 00:48:28.823
like those can, oh, we don't have 65 days

00:48:28.823 --> 00:48:30.953
to shoot this movie we only have 28

00:48:30.953 --> 00:48:32.663
and we're not gonna be able to get this or that.

00:48:32.663 --> 00:48:34.713
And you're forced within those constraints

00:48:34.713 --> 00:48:36.453
to come up with solutions.

00:48:36.453 --> 00:48:41.263
And so often those solutions end up pushing the art forward

00:48:41.263 --> 00:48:43.873
in ways that wouldn't have been possible

00:48:43.873 --> 00:48:44.963
without those constraints.

00:48:44.963 --> 00:48:46.713
So there is something about those boundaries

00:48:46.713 --> 00:48:51.073
I think that really force you to dig deeper

00:48:51.073 --> 00:48:53.433
into your talent and come up with something new.

00:48:53.433 --> 00:48:55.723
And that's how we move culture forward.

00:48:55.723 --> 00:49:00.723
Yes, I mean, we're not anti-meat, but we are pro planet.

00:49:01.993 --> 00:49:06.713
I think that's one thing and if we can prove a way

00:49:06.713 --> 00:49:09.073
that you can be completely without,

00:49:09.073 --> 00:49:12.893
like for example, we feel like we've been working in food

00:49:12.893 --> 00:49:17.043
our whole lives and we're very creative with it.

00:49:17.043 --> 00:49:22.043
And where we apply our creativity, we wanna prove a way

00:49:22.433 --> 00:49:25.053
to do it without animal products.

00:49:25.053 --> 00:49:30.053
If someone orders a cappuccino at the end of their meal,

00:49:30.813 --> 00:49:32.363
we're not gonna lecture you

00:49:32.363 --> 00:49:34.163
what kind of milk you should drink.

00:49:34.163 --> 00:49:36.813
We'll have plenty of plant-based milks,

00:49:36.813 --> 00:49:38.823
but we'll also have the cow milk

00:49:38.823 --> 00:49:41.253
because our we're not the food police.

00:49:41.253 --> 00:49:42.086
You're breaking my heart.

00:49:42.086 --> 00:49:43.133
I know I'm sorry.

00:49:43.133 --> 00:49:47.903
But the most unreal sunflower butter last night,

00:49:47.903 --> 00:49:50.453
and those rolls were extraordinary.

00:49:50.453 --> 00:49:53.433
I can't believe there wasn't dairy in those.

00:49:53.433 --> 00:49:56.393
I have to imagine that you could innovate

00:49:56.393 --> 00:49:59.013
in the plant-based milk space to come up with something

00:49:59.013 --> 00:50:03.233
that would sue the palette and disabuse people of this idea

00:50:03.233 --> 00:50:05.673
that it has to be dairy in their cappuccino.

00:50:05.673 --> 00:50:10.223
We're definitely working on all those kinds of things.

00:50:10.223 --> 00:50:14.513
But we wanna lift people up to come

00:50:14.513 --> 00:50:18.363
because most of our guests are not plant based eaters.

00:50:18.363 --> 00:50:20.363
It's not a vegan restaurant for vegans?

00:50:20.363 --> 00:50:21.196
No.

00:50:21.196 --> 00:50:23.063
All the fancy vegan people are gonna come.

00:50:23.063 --> 00:50:24.063
I know the have been coming.

00:50:24.063 --> 00:50:25.133
Yes.

00:50:25.133 --> 00:50:27.470
But that's not really the intention here.

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:30.383
The intention is to change people's ideas

00:50:30.383 --> 00:50:31.613
of what it could be,

00:50:31.613 --> 00:50:33.823
and that people will look, you know,

00:50:33.823 --> 00:50:35.963
they will eventually make their own decisions

00:50:35.963 --> 00:50:38.283
that they don't want cows milk and their cappuccino.

00:50:38.283 --> 00:50:41.673
Has anybody come to the restaurant who was not aware

00:50:41.673 --> 00:50:44.153
that you'd made this switch to plant-based

00:50:44.153 --> 00:50:45.999
and had some kind of problem.

00:50:45.999 --> 00:50:48.240
(laughing)

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:52.893
As a difficult 1% or it could be?

00:50:52.893 --> 00:50:56.363
No, actually we we've been so blessed

00:50:56.363 --> 00:50:58.803
with so many excited guests.

00:50:58.803 --> 00:51:00.393
I mean, it's been...

00:51:00.393 --> 00:51:02.273
And in a way it's almost like, wow,

00:51:02.273 --> 00:51:04.583
really like everyone is liking it

00:51:04.583 --> 00:51:07.213
because sometimes when you push creativity,

00:51:07.213 --> 00:51:12.213
I'm sure as you know, to a really great place,

00:51:12.643 --> 00:51:15.093
it's not universal.

00:51:15.093 --> 00:51:16.596
Sure.
Liked.

00:51:17.473 --> 00:51:21.323
But somehow this experience, somehow this has hit a nerve

00:51:22.493 --> 00:51:23.973
that was very timely

00:51:23.973 --> 00:51:28.673
and people are really responding extremely positively

00:51:28.673 --> 00:51:30.033
to this experience.

00:51:30.033 --> 00:51:35.033
And even the people who come in with a lot of doubts,

00:51:35.103 --> 00:51:36.563
we definitely had some people

00:51:36.563 --> 00:51:40.481
who made the reservation before COVID and have paid

00:51:40.481 --> 00:51:44.433
before COVID and we're honoring their reservation now,

00:51:44.433 --> 00:51:46.683
but it's obviously a very different question.

00:51:47.563 --> 00:51:50.953
And some of those come in with a lot of reservations,

00:51:50.953 --> 00:51:54.713
but I feel like people are really,

00:51:54.713 --> 00:51:57.013
really living with excitement.

00:51:57.013 --> 00:51:58.343
It's so cool, man.

00:51:58.343 --> 00:52:00.833
Here's my theory, let me know what you think about this.

00:52:00.833 --> 00:52:05.623
I think that you are on this spiritual journey

00:52:06.713 --> 00:52:09.633
towards self-actualization

00:52:09.633 --> 00:52:14.223
and deeper kind of integration within yourself.

00:52:14.223 --> 00:52:17.333
Like you realized that you possess

00:52:17.333 --> 00:52:21.193
a certain level of talent, a certain level of power,

00:52:21.193 --> 00:52:23.563
and the pandemic has forced you

00:52:23.563 --> 00:52:26.343
to try to find deeper meaning in that.

00:52:26.343 --> 00:52:30.173
And you've come out with this marvelous thing

00:52:30.173 --> 00:52:31.686
that you're doing right now.

00:52:32.813 --> 00:52:35.043
And based upon kind of what you shared last night

00:52:35.043 --> 00:52:37.023
about how you feel like you feel lighter,

00:52:37.023 --> 00:52:39.513
you feel more directed, you feel more creative.

00:52:39.513 --> 00:52:42.883
Like there is something to that you know,

00:52:42.883 --> 00:52:47.883
mystical energy of aligning your guests

00:52:48.263 --> 00:52:50.893
with something more meaningful and purposeful

00:52:50.893 --> 00:52:55.873
that creates a more authentic being like a more self

00:52:55.873 --> 00:52:57.953
and an integrated individual,

00:52:57.953 --> 00:53:00.793
and based upon what you were sharing last night,

00:53:00.793 --> 00:53:03.483
this is, I see this transpiring in you.

00:53:03.483 --> 00:53:05.303
And I think it's just,

00:53:05.303 --> 00:53:07.403
I think you'll continue on this journey.

00:53:07.403 --> 00:53:09.063
I'm interested to see where it goes,

00:53:09.063 --> 00:53:10.578
but how do you think about that?

00:53:10.578 --> 00:53:13.316
How do you think about it from like a broader,

00:53:14.287 --> 00:53:15.383
more like mystical perspective?

00:53:15.383 --> 00:53:18.642
I know you're Swiss, you're precise and you know.

00:53:18.642 --> 00:53:20.513
(laughing)

00:53:20.513 --> 00:53:22.283
So I don't know what your relationship.

00:53:22.283 --> 00:53:24.696
I don't know where you're going this,

00:53:25.892 --> 00:53:27.289
but I kind of know.

00:53:27.289 --> 00:53:28.880
You know, nothing is like,

00:53:28.880 --> 00:53:31.616
that's what makes it interesting being Swiss.

00:53:32.833 --> 00:53:35.236
It must be confusing for you, right?

00:53:36.103 --> 00:53:37.475
So I can...

00:53:37.475 --> 00:53:38.503
I can calibrate your watch to this.

00:53:38.503 --> 00:53:39.903
I know, know you can.

00:53:41.023 --> 00:53:46.023
I definitely, you know, after reaching the mountain top

00:53:47.903 --> 00:53:50.576
of becoming the best restaurant in the world,

00:53:51.593 --> 00:53:52.863
at a pretty young age,

00:53:52.863 --> 00:53:56.193
I definitely asked myself a lot of questions

00:53:56.193 --> 00:53:58.513
and I've done a lot of soul searching

00:53:58.513 --> 00:54:02.196
and I've tried all kinds of things to soul search.

00:54:03.783 --> 00:54:07.183
I went to India for two months,

00:54:07.183 --> 00:54:09.393
which was incredible and I felt

00:54:12.867 --> 00:54:14.223
it was challenging also.

00:54:14.223 --> 00:54:15.683
I don't know if you've been to India.

00:54:15.683 --> 00:54:17.073
I haven't no, my wife has.

00:54:17.073 --> 00:54:20.323
It's an incredible place and a challenging place.

00:54:20.323 --> 00:54:23.573
And you feel completely out of your comfort zone,

00:54:23.573 --> 00:54:26.633
but it's extremely spiritual as well.

00:54:26.633 --> 00:54:31.633
And I've done a few psychedelic journeys as well,

00:54:32.433 --> 00:54:37.290
and, you know, just searching for more meaning

00:54:39.223 --> 00:54:42.643
and for a fuller version of myself.

00:54:42.643 --> 00:54:46.573
And I think, and I hope it's just gonna keep growing,

00:54:46.573 --> 00:54:50.503
but today the work is definitely the most meaningful

00:54:50.503 --> 00:54:54.023
and it feels like so many parts of my life

00:54:54.023 --> 00:54:55.104
have led to this right moment.

00:54:55.104 --> 00:54:58.553
Yeah, you told me last night that

00:54:58.553 --> 00:55:01.913
you're almost quick to say that you're not vegan yourself

00:55:02.993 --> 00:55:05.335
despite you know what you're doing in the restaurant.

00:55:05.335 --> 00:55:08.507
And I think I said something like, well,

00:55:08.507 --> 00:55:13.507
if you can dispense with that, you know, disconnect,

00:55:14.243 --> 00:55:17.123
like if you can align your personal lifestyle

00:55:17.123 --> 00:55:19.263
with what you're doing professionally,

00:55:19.263 --> 00:55:23.863
I think you'll see an even greater kind of growth

00:55:23.863 --> 00:55:26.393
or kind of celebration of what you're doing,

00:55:26.393 --> 00:55:29.353
but I think you'll also feel more integrated

00:55:29.353 --> 00:55:31.803
and purposeful in what you're doing.

00:55:31.803 --> 00:55:35.293
I think you're probably right,

00:55:35.293 --> 00:55:38.363
but the way I've always kind of lived my life

00:55:38.363 --> 00:55:40.343
is more out of intuition

00:55:41.413 --> 00:55:46.003
rather than so much about what I'm thinking.

00:55:46.003 --> 00:55:48.513
I should do more my gut feeling.

00:55:48.513 --> 00:55:52.893
And I think this has all come from that place

00:55:52.893 --> 00:55:56.063
that I just felt like this is the right thing to do.

00:55:56.063 --> 00:55:58.322
And I think my life just needs to catch up on

00:55:58.322 --> 00:55:59.847
all of it's happening.

00:55:59.847 --> 00:56:02.219
I think it's inevitable that instinct

00:56:02.219 --> 00:56:04.703
that intuition will evolve as well.

00:56:04.703 --> 00:56:06.673
It's absolutely happening.

00:56:06.673 --> 00:56:11.673
I mean, even, you know, it will be strange for me

00:56:12.293 --> 00:56:16.273
at this point to eat a plate of meat.

00:56:16.273 --> 00:56:19.803
I haven't done it and it's almost the body's telling me,

00:56:19.803 --> 00:56:22.383
you know, what the body's telling you

00:56:22.383 --> 00:56:23.353
what's actually good for you.

00:56:23.353 --> 00:56:26.843
Yeah, well, what happens is, as you continue to evolve

00:56:26.843 --> 00:56:29.783
and grow those things become less appealing

00:56:29.783 --> 00:56:32.253
and they just fall away effortlessly

00:56:32.253 --> 00:56:34.673
as opposed to like, okay, now I'm gonna try really hard

00:56:34.673 --> 00:56:37.503
to do this thing it just becomes incompatible

00:56:37.503 --> 00:56:38.963
with the way that you're living.

00:56:38.963 --> 00:56:41.413
Yeah, no and you know, and it's...

00:56:41.413 --> 00:56:45.213
I do believe into talking things into existence

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:49.346
when I have some big ideas I just started talking about

00:56:49.346 --> 00:56:54.346
even before I even fully comprehend what that means.

00:56:54.903 --> 00:56:56.296
I mean, to--

00:56:56.296 --> 00:56:57.813
It makes him real.

00:56:57.813 --> 00:56:59.806
It does make him real, but also,

00:57:00.773 --> 00:57:02.303
you know, it's a really scary thing.

00:57:02.303 --> 00:57:06.833
Change is scary for a lot of people and I'm not in a,

00:57:06.833 --> 00:57:09.223
you know, I'm in a team sport,

00:57:09.223 --> 00:57:14.223
like there's a whole army to make this happen every day.

00:57:14.723 --> 00:57:16.753
And it's not just what's in my head,

00:57:16.753 --> 00:57:20.653
but it's like, I need to get everyone else on board.

00:57:20.653 --> 00:57:23.293
Like what happened when you told your investors

00:57:23.293 --> 00:57:25.846
are Like, this is what I'm gonna do.

00:57:28.073 --> 00:57:30.613
No, he always says go, no there's,

00:57:30.613 --> 00:57:35.434
so our building, we have a lease in our building

00:57:35.434 --> 00:57:40.434
and it's owned by two different landlords.

00:57:40.723 --> 00:57:42.590
I think when they bought the building few years ago,

00:57:42.590 --> 00:57:45.503
it's like a billion dollar building or something like that.

00:57:45.503 --> 00:57:47.323
And they're 50/50 partners.

00:57:47.323 --> 00:57:50.453
And one of the partners reached out to the other partner

00:57:50.453 --> 00:57:52.693
when they heard about my decision.

00:57:52.693 --> 00:57:56.653
And they said, well, do we have any say in this

00:57:56.653 --> 00:58:00.503
as the landlord of the building

00:58:00.503 --> 00:58:01.850
I mean--
Oh, my God.

00:58:03.125 --> 00:58:04.152
It was hard.

00:58:04.152 --> 00:58:05.564
Yeah.
Obviously they don't,

00:58:05.564 --> 00:58:10.564
but people really, it is hard for people to deal with change

00:58:10.923 --> 00:58:12.193
and our team as well.

00:58:12.193 --> 00:58:17.193
I mean, it was hard for our team to deal with this.

00:58:17.681 --> 00:58:18.514
Yeah, it's a big deal.

00:58:18.514 --> 00:58:19.356
It's scary though.

00:58:19.356 --> 00:58:20.583
It's scary.

00:58:20.583 --> 00:58:23.176
Well you don't get to this point,

00:58:24.083 --> 00:58:28.473
you know, sitting at top of the pinnacle of cuisine

00:58:28.473 --> 00:58:32.423
in the manner that you do without being competitive,

00:58:32.423 --> 00:58:35.753
like behind your, you know, very charming disposition.

00:58:35.753 --> 00:58:37.823
Like there's gotta be a killer in there,

00:58:37.823 --> 00:58:38.927
right?
Yeah.

00:58:38.927 --> 00:58:41.803
Like, and you're somebody who was a very good athlete.

00:58:41.803 --> 00:58:43.663
Like I'm interested, let's go back.

00:58:43.663 --> 00:58:47.033
Cause the origin story is crazy with you

00:58:47.033 --> 00:58:49.403
growing up in a household where you had

00:58:50.633 --> 00:58:53.953
a challenging, difficult relationship with your dad,

00:58:53.953 --> 00:58:56.583
your mom introducing you to food at a young age.

00:58:56.583 --> 00:58:58.243
But walk me through

00:58:58.243 --> 00:59:01.303
what it was like growing up in Switzerland as a kid.

00:59:01.303 --> 00:59:06.043
Yeah, I grew up with to as the youngest child

00:59:06.043 --> 00:59:09.296
to my parents who were very young at that time,

00:59:10.373 --> 00:59:14.252
my dad was going to architecture school,

00:59:14.252 --> 00:59:19.066
you know, super detail oriented,

00:59:20.033 --> 00:59:24.466
but tough, difficult dad and nothing was ever good enough.

00:59:25.363 --> 00:59:30.183
And it was hard and I think that to the point

00:59:30.183 --> 00:59:34.553
where you said earlier when there's yelling in the kitchen,

00:59:34.553 --> 00:59:37.203
you know, there was yelling at home sometimes.

00:59:37.203 --> 00:59:39.303
And it was also because it was just,

00:59:39.303 --> 00:59:40.623
everything was overwhelming.

00:59:40.623 --> 00:59:42.803
I mean, my dad was like, you know,

00:59:42.803 --> 00:59:46.963
21 years old and there I was born

00:59:46.963 --> 00:59:49.153
and he was also going to architecture school

00:59:49.153 --> 00:59:53.323
and, you know, I think it was, is difficult.

00:59:53.323 --> 00:59:58.323
And it was, I started biking a lot.

00:59:59.383 --> 01:00:00.216
First--

01:00:00.216 --> 01:00:01.153
You were runners up.

01:00:01.153 --> 01:00:05.353
First I ran and sport for me was sort of the outlet.

01:00:05.353 --> 01:00:08.263
I mean, the more time I could be out of the house,

01:00:08.263 --> 01:00:12.423
the happier I was, I didn't really love school that much,

01:00:12.423 --> 01:00:13.933
but I started running

01:00:13.933 --> 01:00:16.793
and I started running even as a young kid, like for miles

01:00:16.793 --> 01:00:18.353
and miles and miles.

01:00:18.353 --> 01:00:22.733
And I competed in the, in cross-country

01:00:23.973 --> 01:00:28.053
and that gave me the opportunity to travel around,

01:00:28.053 --> 01:00:29.423
which was really exciting

01:00:29.423 --> 01:00:31.726
when you're like 10, 11, 12 years old.

01:00:32.953 --> 01:00:37.803
And I was running, I won the nationals two years in a row

01:00:37.803 --> 01:00:41.504
and at a very young age as a junior,

01:00:41.504 --> 01:00:44.523
but then, and then even running

01:00:44.523 --> 01:00:46.583
wasn't even got me far enough.

01:00:46.583 --> 01:00:50.223
So then I started biking because then I could do in travel,

01:00:50.223 --> 01:00:51.451
you know, all--

01:00:51.451 --> 01:00:53.572
But the dad, your dad wasn't giving you the love

01:00:53.572 --> 01:00:54.405
on the running?

01:00:54.405 --> 01:00:55.545
No love on the running.

01:00:55.545 --> 01:00:56.843
No love?
No love on the running.

01:00:56.843 --> 01:01:01.843
You know, when I could come in first

01:01:01.883 --> 01:01:04.953
and his response will be,

01:01:04.953 --> 01:01:09.493
well, the best guys were injured or something like that.

01:01:09.493 --> 01:01:12.733
You know, the guys who would have won were injured.

01:01:12.733 --> 01:01:14.613
It was never really,

01:01:14.613 --> 01:01:17.006
the winds were in really ever celebrated.

01:01:18.023 --> 01:01:20.193
It was hard man sorry man.
It's hard.

01:01:20.193 --> 01:01:23.103
It's, it is interesting that, you know,

01:01:23.103 --> 01:01:25.773
I didn't have that specific experience,

01:01:25.773 --> 01:01:27.953
but I did grow up underneath a dad

01:01:27.953 --> 01:01:30.303
who set very high expectations for me

01:01:30.303 --> 01:01:34.273
that I couldn't ever seem to quite satisfy.

01:01:34.273 --> 01:01:36.413
And I've had to do a lot of work around that

01:01:36.413 --> 01:01:38.603
to make peace with it and I love my dad

01:01:38.603 --> 01:01:40.743
and, you know, he did the best he could

01:01:40.743 --> 01:01:43.763
and he's from a different generation, et cetera.

01:01:43.763 --> 01:01:46.103
But I think there's something about growing up

01:01:46.103 --> 01:01:49.423
with that kind of parenting

01:01:49.423 --> 01:01:53.793
or experiencing that kind of trauma that implants in you,

01:01:53.793 --> 01:01:57.543
this competitiveness like this drive to excel

01:01:57.543 --> 01:02:01.533
because you so desperately wanna be recognized and seen.

01:02:01.533 --> 01:02:04.643
And I feel myself to this day still, you know,

01:02:04.643 --> 01:02:08.403
I rely on that as a strength that can also lead me astray,

01:02:08.403 --> 01:02:10.373
but it is in some respect,

01:02:10.373 --> 01:02:12.463
like a super power that rested within me

01:02:12.463 --> 01:02:15.423
because that was born out of that type of experience.

01:02:15.423 --> 01:02:19.803
Yeah, that will be my dad's response to this.

01:02:19.803 --> 01:02:21.238
How good, you know.

01:02:21.238 --> 01:02:23.488
(laughing)

01:02:24.555 --> 01:02:26.303
So then you get on a bike and then it's freedom

01:02:26.303 --> 01:02:27.136
I'm outta here.

01:02:27.136 --> 01:02:29.103
I can be gone like all day.

01:02:29.103 --> 01:02:33.523
Yeah, I started racing and I started, you know,

01:02:33.523 --> 01:02:36.460
pretty much winning a lot of races early.

01:02:36.460 --> 01:02:41.460
And so I had the opportunity to leave school at 14 years old

01:02:42.273 --> 01:02:45.643
to really become a professional cyclist,

01:02:45.643 --> 01:02:50.643
to be part of a junior program for a serious team.

01:02:51.073 --> 01:02:55.093
And for me it was like a no brainer.

01:02:55.093 --> 01:02:57.713
I could leave home and school.

01:02:57.713 --> 01:03:00.073
It was like a greatest I wanted to do it.

01:03:00.073 --> 01:03:02.116
And so, yeah, that was amazing.

01:03:02.116 --> 01:03:04.023
What did dad think about that?

01:03:04.023 --> 01:03:05.926
Oh, it was so tough.

01:03:06.913 --> 01:03:08.903
He didn't think it was a good idea at all,

01:03:08.903 --> 01:03:10.813
but somehow I got away with it

01:03:10.813 --> 01:03:15.493
and I went for it and that I did leave school at 14.

01:03:15.493 --> 01:03:17.973
So, that's the last formal schooling that you had?

01:03:17.973 --> 01:03:20.253
Yeah, I went to school for eight years of my life.

01:03:20.253 --> 01:03:21.273
Wow, and mom?

01:03:21.273 --> 01:03:24.753
I mean, she was crying

01:03:24.753 --> 01:03:26.563
when I was leaving home,

01:03:26.563 --> 01:03:31.293
because my mom, I was very, very close to her

01:03:31.293 --> 01:03:34.423
and she was sort of like magical in Chicago

01:03:34.423 --> 01:03:37.753
and spiritual as well.

01:03:37.753 --> 01:03:42.633
Like she's an artist and for her it was just heartbreaking

01:03:43.583 --> 01:03:45.443
for me to leave at that age.

01:03:45.443 --> 01:03:49.423
But looking back at it, it was the best decision.

01:03:49.423 --> 01:03:53.253
It actually suited me to go down this path.

01:03:53.253 --> 01:03:54.895
Are they still around now?

01:03:54.895 --> 01:03:57.263
Yeah.
How's it going now?

01:03:57.263 --> 01:03:59.894
Well, my mom passed away, unfortunately.

01:03:59.894 --> 01:04:00.727
Oh, I'm sorry.

01:04:00.727 --> 01:04:02.683
Yeah, but my dad is around and you know,

01:04:02.683 --> 01:04:05.163
I have an okay relationship

01:04:05.163 --> 01:04:09.543
I think I agree I do love him too.

01:04:09.543 --> 01:04:12.136
And also he did the best he could,

01:04:13.913 --> 01:04:18.876
but it's hard to win when you are not supported at that age.

01:04:20.503 --> 01:04:23.133
It's hard to ever fully come clear with it.

01:04:23.133 --> 01:04:24.533
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:04:24.533 --> 01:04:27.293
It's tough, so you, you leave home at 15?

01:04:27.293 --> 01:04:28.503
No.
And that was it?

01:04:28.503 --> 01:04:30.045
No.

01:04:30.045 --> 01:04:32.503
And go be a bicycle racer.

01:04:32.503 --> 01:04:33.949
Yeah, it was amazing.

01:04:33.949 --> 01:04:35.780
What you did for many years right?

01:04:35.780 --> 01:04:38.193
It was amazing I got to see all of Europe

01:04:38.193 --> 01:04:42.383
and it taught me so, so, so much.

01:04:42.383 --> 01:04:44.943
And I would never be the chef today

01:04:45.883 --> 01:04:49.423
if I didn't have that as a background, as an athlete,

01:04:49.423 --> 01:04:51.533
you know, like there's so many setbacks

01:04:51.533 --> 01:04:56.533
and it is so tough and so competitive and--

01:04:56.753 --> 01:05:00.563
There's this romantic idea around being a cyclist,

01:05:00.563 --> 01:05:03.863
a professional cyclist, but the reality is so brutal.

01:05:03.863 --> 01:05:04.950
There is no brutal.

01:05:04.950 --> 01:05:06.143
You making no money.
Yeah.

01:05:06.143 --> 01:05:08.243
You're living in your Belgium

01:05:08.243 --> 01:05:10.116
or something middle of the winter

01:05:10.116 --> 01:05:14.533
and terrible weather freezing all the time.

01:05:14.533 --> 01:05:17.223
Eating raw manner or whatever,

01:05:17.223 --> 01:05:19.923
barely making it enough to scrape by

01:05:19.923 --> 01:05:22.437
and always worried about getting cut from the team.

01:05:22.437 --> 01:05:26.173
Getting cut from the team, you know,

01:05:26.173 --> 01:05:28.153
injuries are all the time.

01:05:28.153 --> 01:05:32.833
Crashes, injuries it's just, yeah.

01:05:32.833 --> 01:05:37.193
Eventually I had a bad accident.

01:05:37.193 --> 01:05:38.693
I was 22 years old

01:05:38.693 --> 01:05:43.013
and that kind of put everything in perspective for me

01:05:43.013 --> 01:05:48.013
in a way grateful to that accident because I was in,

01:05:48.658 --> 01:05:53.658
in the hospital for weeks and I realized that

01:05:54.683 --> 01:05:56.961
this isn't going anywhere.

01:05:56.961 --> 01:06:00.583
I was not on the past to be Lance Armstrong.

01:06:00.583 --> 01:06:03.673
And there were only like two or three guys

01:06:03.673 --> 01:06:07.666
on that level who actually made a living from it.

01:06:08.623 --> 01:06:13.103
And that was in something that was in the cards for me.

01:06:13.103 --> 01:06:18.103
And we also know about those times in cycling.

01:06:19.503 --> 01:06:21.423
So it was just that kind of environment

01:06:21.423 --> 01:06:23.653
that was very challenging and difficult.

01:06:23.653 --> 01:06:27.033
And it actually only worked for a few

01:06:27.033 --> 01:06:28.853
and not even in the long run.

01:06:28.853 --> 01:06:31.023
Yeah, I mean, you have these very interesting

01:06:31.023 --> 01:06:33.353
inflection points throughout your life

01:06:33.353 --> 01:06:37.083
that are all defined by these 180 pivots that you make

01:06:37.083 --> 01:06:39.243
from leaving home at 15 and saying,

01:06:39.243 --> 01:06:42.693
I'm not doing school anymore to having the self-awareness

01:06:42.693 --> 01:06:44.313
as a young person how old were you then?

01:06:44.313 --> 01:06:46.753
18 or 21 or something like that.

01:06:46.753 --> 01:06:48.523
When you have that crash.
21.

01:06:48.523 --> 01:06:51.883
21 right, to say, this is not gonna work

01:06:51.883 --> 01:06:54.360
and I'm gonna completely let it go.

01:06:54.360 --> 01:06:59.148
That requires a certain level of maturity to do that.

01:06:59.148 --> 01:07:00.603
It would have been easy to just keep going

01:07:00.603 --> 01:07:04.313
and let it play out to its inevitable conclusion.

01:07:04.313 --> 01:07:05.923
But to say, I'm pulling out of this

01:07:05.923 --> 01:07:08.733
and I'm go gonna a completely new direction.

01:07:08.733 --> 01:07:09.852
I think I'm--

01:07:09.852 --> 01:07:11.923
It's the same thing when you decide

01:07:11.923 --> 01:07:14.423
to make 11 Madison Park plant-based

01:07:14.423 --> 01:07:19.313
like these very like hard line in the sand switch ups.

01:07:19.313 --> 01:07:22.883
It's so liberating when you do though.

01:07:22.883 --> 01:07:27.883
And it comes when I moved to America when I was 25

01:07:28.123 --> 01:07:31.793
and I just left there was kind of a same similar moment

01:07:31.793 --> 01:07:33.593
where I just left everything behind.

01:07:33.593 --> 01:07:35.273
And I came with two suitcases

01:07:35.273 --> 01:07:37.993
and I barely spoke the language,

01:07:37.993 --> 01:07:41.413
but it is such a liberating feeling

01:07:41.413 --> 01:07:45.893
to know that everything you need is inside of you.

01:07:45.893 --> 01:07:47.683
You don't need anything else.

01:07:47.683 --> 01:07:52.683
And I mean, only COVID could have given me this experience

01:07:53.443 --> 01:07:58.443
again, so late in life at 44 years old,

01:07:58.853 --> 01:08:03.513
where I was literally sitting in my apartment a year ago,

01:08:03.513 --> 01:08:05.993
not knowing if I'm gonna have a restaurant,

01:08:05.993 --> 01:08:10.853
not knowing how to pay for the bills for the business

01:08:10.853 --> 01:08:15.853
and coming to terms with the idea I might lose it all

01:08:18.823 --> 01:08:19.746
and it's okay.

01:08:20.693 --> 01:08:24.656
And when you get to that place and I hope I can,

01:08:25.973 --> 01:08:28.658
I hope I don't need to get physically to that place,

01:08:28.658 --> 01:08:31.913
you know, again, but I hope mentally

01:08:31.913 --> 01:08:35.443
I can get myself into that space

01:08:35.443 --> 01:08:39.393
for many, many years to come because in that space

01:08:39.393 --> 01:08:42.503
you're very free and you're able

01:08:42.503 --> 01:08:46.693
to make pretty bold moves without fear.

01:08:46.693 --> 01:08:48.403
But you did your work,

01:08:48.403 --> 01:08:50.693
like you've been doing pushups all along.

01:08:50.693 --> 01:08:52.813
Like you've made these pivots in the past,

01:08:52.813 --> 01:08:54.413
you've taken these risks.

01:08:54.413 --> 01:08:56.953
It's worked out for better or worse each time.

01:08:56.953 --> 01:08:59.433
So when it comes to making this big,

01:08:59.433 --> 01:09:01.193
more recent decision that you've made,

01:09:01.193 --> 01:09:04.903
you already have that life experience of knowing

01:09:04.903 --> 01:09:06.083
this is the only way

01:09:06.083 --> 01:09:08.223
because this is the way I've always done it.

01:09:08.223 --> 01:09:09.753
And somehow it always works out.

01:09:09.753 --> 01:09:12.883
Maybe not in the manner in which I imagine

01:09:12.883 --> 01:09:16.503
around the timetable, but short of doing that,

01:09:16.503 --> 01:09:20.714
you rob yourself off the magic that can transpire

01:09:20.714 --> 01:09:24.103
by deciding to incur that kind of risk.

01:09:24.103 --> 01:09:26.693
Yeah, I think it only always works out.

01:09:26.693 --> 01:09:31.693
Not because there's this success that's by your audience,

01:09:32.833 --> 01:09:35.753
but it's the success that's you do,

01:09:35.753 --> 01:09:39.353
what's in your heart and that's why it works out.

01:09:39.353 --> 01:09:42.193
Like when you do what's in your heart,

01:09:42.193 --> 01:09:43.853
then it's the right decision.

01:09:43.853 --> 01:09:47.563
It doesn't matter if it's critically successful or not.

01:09:47.563 --> 01:09:50.433
But if you continue to follow your heart,

01:09:50.433 --> 01:09:53.719
I believe that gives you freedom and that, you know.

01:09:53.719 --> 01:09:58.013
Yeah, yeah, so in the wake of the bicycle accident at 21,

01:09:58.013 --> 01:10:00.120
walk me through the introduction to food

01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:02.993
and that becoming like your path.

01:10:02.993 --> 01:10:06.223
Well, as you said, as a cyclist,

01:10:06.223 --> 01:10:08.783
you don't make a lot of money and I didn't either.

01:10:08.783 --> 01:10:13.783
And so actually at 15, when I decided to go pursue cycling

01:10:16.583 --> 01:10:20.360
as a profession my dad said, "Okay, you can do this,

01:10:20.360 --> 01:10:22.360
"but you have to move out.

01:10:22.360 --> 01:10:24.663
"I'm not supporting this."

01:10:24.663 --> 01:10:28.793
And I said, okay, I'm not even fully understanding

01:10:28.793 --> 01:10:33.283
what that meant but I had to get a job.

01:10:33.283 --> 01:10:36.863
So when I was in training or traveling for races,

01:10:36.863 --> 01:10:38.053
I had to find a job.

01:10:38.053 --> 01:10:41.673
And the only place I could get a job was in a kitchen

01:10:41.673 --> 01:10:42.883
at 15 years old.

01:10:42.883 --> 01:10:46.853
And that was really lucky that I found a place

01:10:46.853 --> 01:10:50.393
with a chef that became my mentor.

01:10:50.393 --> 01:10:55.393
And it wasn't just a job, but he took it on him to teach me.

01:10:57.223 --> 01:10:58.433
comes like a father figure.

01:10:58.433 --> 01:11:00.653
He was like a father figure.

01:11:00.653 --> 01:11:03.333
And by the time the accident happened,

01:11:03.333 --> 01:11:06.383
I've actually already cooked for six years.

01:11:06.383 --> 01:11:09.923
And my mom was an excellent cook

01:11:09.923 --> 01:11:14.923
and even more so she really put a lot of value

01:11:16.823 --> 01:11:18.183
on the ingredients.

01:11:18.183 --> 01:11:22.083
Like we got all of our vegetables from the farm,

01:11:22.083 --> 01:11:24.813
everything from the farm, like fresh milk every day,

01:11:24.813 --> 01:11:26.586
she would bake her own breads.

01:11:27.513 --> 01:11:30.233
We would pick our own salads in the fields.

01:11:30.233 --> 01:11:33.743
And so, you know, as a kid that was annoying,

01:11:33.743 --> 01:11:36.643
but because so much work to prepare the meal,

01:11:36.643 --> 01:11:38.273
but it gave me this understanding

01:11:38.273 --> 01:11:40.843
of what a great ingredient was.

01:11:40.843 --> 01:11:44.023
So in a way I was set up for it without even knowing,

01:11:44.023 --> 01:11:47.963
I never thought I would pursue cooking as a career,

01:11:47.963 --> 01:11:49.903
but when I was in the hospital

01:11:51.163 --> 01:11:53.353
and I thought about the cycling,

01:11:53.353 --> 01:11:56.243
I actually didn't like cycling as much anymore.

01:11:56.243 --> 01:11:58.443
It just became something so different.

01:11:58.443 --> 01:12:01.346
They became so, so, so competitive.

01:12:02.193 --> 01:12:05.313
And it wasn't enjoyable.

01:12:05.313 --> 01:12:08.583
And when I thought about where I found pleasure

01:12:08.583 --> 01:12:12.693
was when I thought of the moments in the kitchen,

01:12:12.693 --> 01:12:14.930
and then I thought well, I should just do that.

01:12:14.930 --> 01:12:19.930
And if I can't become the best cyclist in the world,

01:12:20.083 --> 01:12:23.203
maybe I can become one of the best chefs.

01:12:23.203 --> 01:12:26.483
Was that at a very concrete decision that you made?

01:12:26.483 --> 01:12:27.673
Like do you have awareness

01:12:27.673 --> 01:12:29.403
that you were making that kind of pivot

01:12:29.403 --> 01:12:33.246
and refocusing all of your intentionality into this?

01:12:33.246 --> 01:12:35.173
Okay, okay, I was all in on cycling

01:12:35.173 --> 01:12:36.813
now I'm going all in on this.

01:12:36.813 --> 01:12:38.703
Yeah, this happened in one day

01:12:38.703 --> 01:12:39.536
Wow.

01:12:39.536 --> 01:12:42.963
This was one moment when I made that decision.

01:12:42.963 --> 01:12:47.963
And then one of my trainers was close to this restaurant

01:12:49.573 --> 01:12:51.073
that had three stars

01:12:51.953 --> 01:12:53.933
it was in the French part of Switzerland.

01:12:53.933 --> 01:12:58.093
And we ate there once after a race and it was so magical.

01:12:58.093 --> 01:13:01.043
Like we, we sat in that kitchen

01:13:01.043 --> 01:13:04.283
and that was actually the first time

01:13:04.283 --> 01:13:07.223
I saw cooking on this level.

01:13:07.223 --> 01:13:10.063
That was actually that kind of plant that the seed

01:13:10.913 --> 01:13:15.330
that cooking could be a sport or career,

01:13:17.113 --> 01:13:19.313
but we were sitting in the middle of this three

01:13:19.313 --> 01:13:21.443
Michelin star restaurant.

01:13:21.443 --> 01:13:23.266
It was called Freddie Sheraday.

01:13:24.272 --> 01:13:29.240
And they were like 25 chefs working all around us.

01:13:30.593 --> 01:13:33.433
Like similar to what you saw last night at our restaurant.

01:13:33.433 --> 01:13:37.633
But everyone had perfect starched uniforms

01:13:37.633 --> 01:13:40.783
and big tokes the hats.

01:13:40.783 --> 01:13:44.583
And they were serving us the most incredible meal.

01:13:44.583 --> 01:13:46.823
And they were working in harmony

01:13:46.823 --> 01:13:49.263
and it was like watching a balley

01:13:49.263 --> 01:13:52.063
and sort of that planted a seed

01:13:52.063 --> 01:13:57.063
and yeah, I was in the hospital and I called my coach

01:13:57.363 --> 01:14:00.073
and I said, do you remember that dinner

01:14:00.073 --> 01:14:03.653
we had two years ago can you call that guy?

01:14:03.653 --> 01:14:05.273
I wanna work there.

01:14:05.273 --> 01:14:07.523
And he said, what?

01:14:07.523 --> 01:14:10.430
He's just like, well, I don't wanna race anymore.

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:11.730
-You're baring the lead.

01:14:14.043 --> 01:14:15.843
And he did it and he did.

01:14:15.843 --> 01:14:18.273
And then three months later I had a job.

01:14:18.273 --> 01:14:20.753
Wow, Wow.

01:14:20.753 --> 01:14:21.853
And is that the restaurant?

01:14:21.853 --> 01:14:25.273
What was the restaurant that you were working in

01:14:25.273 --> 01:14:30.273
when you had the accident on route to the farmer's market

01:14:30.443 --> 01:14:31.893
at like three in the morning?

01:14:32.743 --> 01:14:33.873
That was later, right?

01:14:33.873 --> 01:14:37.605
That was later, that was a few years later.

01:14:37.605 --> 01:14:41.323
I was working in this little restaurant.

01:14:41.323 --> 01:14:45.053
That was actually my first place where I was the chef

01:14:45.053 --> 01:14:50.053
and I was 25 years old and I would go to the market

01:14:51.423 --> 01:14:54.103
every morning to Sarek it was an hour away

01:14:54.103 --> 01:14:56.403
and this was in a mountain town.

01:14:56.403 --> 01:14:58.493
And one morning there was so much snow

01:14:58.493 --> 01:15:01.933
and I was so tired probably slept four hours.

01:15:01.933 --> 01:15:05.223
And I got up at five in the morning to drive to Sarek

01:15:05.223 --> 01:15:09.443
and yeah, and the car just went out of control

01:15:09.443 --> 01:15:14.443
and I was sledding down this hill and I hit a tree

01:15:14.893 --> 01:15:17.256
and I was super lucky that there was a tree.

01:15:19.013 --> 01:15:20.913
Cause short of you angled the car

01:15:20.913 --> 01:15:23.773
towards the little twig of a tree, because short of that,

01:15:23.773 --> 01:15:26.833
you were looking at an Alpine cliff that it would have

01:15:26.833 --> 01:15:28.163
certainly met your death.

01:15:28.163 --> 01:15:29.633
Yeah, it would have killed me.

01:15:29.633 --> 01:15:30.723
Wow.

01:15:30.723 --> 01:15:33.363
And then I called the farmer of this place

01:15:33.363 --> 01:15:36.663
and he came with the tractor and he pulled me out.

01:15:36.663 --> 01:15:40.933
Like the thing, man, I need to reassess my priorities

01:15:40.933 --> 01:15:43.163
instead you're like, I gotta get to the farmer's market

01:15:43.163 --> 01:15:44.661
because the restaurant can't operate.

01:15:44.661 --> 01:15:46.973
Once I get there and get the stuff.

01:15:46.973 --> 01:15:51.973
Like the level of obsession at that moment in your life

01:15:53.213 --> 01:15:55.953
layered on top of which you've got a very complicated

01:15:55.953 --> 01:15:59.363
personal situation you had a kid at 18.

01:15:59.363 --> 01:16:02.663
Like you had an older girlfriend who then left you,

01:16:02.663 --> 01:16:05.053
which you know, the pain of that forces you

01:16:05.053 --> 01:16:07.103
to just go all in on career.

01:16:07.103 --> 01:16:08.173
I won't be hurt.

01:16:08.173 --> 01:16:09.313
Nobody can abandon me.

01:16:09.313 --> 01:16:11.213
This is the one thing that I can control

01:16:11.213 --> 01:16:13.553
and just literally becoming obsessed.

01:16:13.553 --> 01:16:14.653
Yeah, I know that's right.

01:16:14.653 --> 01:16:19.653
Yeah, it's not always healthy, but it definitely pushes you.

01:16:20.933 --> 01:16:25.933
It definitely, yeah, you have to push yourself

01:16:27.303 --> 01:16:30.963
to extreme levels to reach the success.

01:16:30.963 --> 01:16:33.651
And that's what you learn in sports.

01:16:33.651 --> 01:16:34.883
It's--
100%.

01:16:34.883 --> 01:16:36.473
Not who is the most talented

01:16:36.473 --> 01:16:39.533
is like who can hurt the most actually.

01:16:39.533 --> 01:16:40.913
Well, especially in cycling

01:16:40.913 --> 01:16:41.803
Yeah.

01:16:41.803 --> 01:16:44.663
Because nothing, there's no sort of self-inflicted

01:16:44.663 --> 01:16:47.783
suffering can be experienced like it can be on a bike.

01:16:47.783 --> 01:16:48.883
Yeah, I know, that's right.

01:16:48.883 --> 01:16:50.653
And you learn that.

01:16:50.653 --> 01:16:55.653
But this then gets channeled into this discussion around

01:16:55.673 --> 01:16:57.483
passion like we live in a culture,

01:16:57.483 --> 01:16:58.873
it's like chase your passion.

01:16:58.873 --> 01:17:00.723
We should all live passionate lives.

01:17:00.723 --> 01:17:03.905
And what I love about the way that you think about this

01:17:03.905 --> 01:17:08.253
is the fact that it's filtered through the German

01:17:08.253 --> 01:17:11.673
definition of passion, this idea of lightened shaft.

01:17:11.673 --> 01:17:15.113
So explain that because I think it's really profound

01:17:15.113 --> 01:17:17.513
and it's so much of a better way

01:17:17.513 --> 01:17:21.383
to think about this type of lifestyle.

01:17:21.383 --> 01:17:25.413
Yeah, no, in German, the word passion is light and shaft

01:17:25.413 --> 01:17:28.613
and light and is actually suffering

01:17:28.613 --> 01:17:33.613
and shaft is sort of like enduring,

01:17:33.613 --> 01:17:35.423
you know, endure, suffering,

01:17:35.423 --> 01:17:38.103
or being willing to suffer for something

01:17:38.103 --> 01:17:39.983
because that's really passion.

01:17:39.983 --> 01:17:42.553
Otherwise it's just like the hobby,

01:17:42.553 --> 01:17:45.823
but if you're passionate about something,

01:17:45.823 --> 01:17:49.223
you know, are you willing to suffer for it?

01:17:49.223 --> 01:17:51.833
Yeah, exactly.

01:17:51.833 --> 01:17:54.583
And the suffering will inevitably come.

01:17:54.583 --> 01:17:56.913
It will be visited upon you

01:17:56.913 --> 01:18:01.913
because doing anything well is fucking is hard.

01:18:02.473 --> 01:18:05.263
And if you wanna be at the highest level,

01:18:05.263 --> 01:18:08.103
prepare yourself, like there just aren't that many people

01:18:08.103 --> 01:18:11.293
who are willing to kind of endure what it's going to take

01:18:11.293 --> 01:18:14.903
to get to the peak, whether it's in cycling

01:18:14.903 --> 01:18:19.793
or running or being a chef or any other goal or dream

01:18:19.793 --> 01:18:21.923
that you're chasing, that's just the truth.

01:18:21.923 --> 01:18:25.733
But it's the journey that is beautiful

01:18:25.733 --> 01:18:26.913
and there is suffering,

01:18:26.913 --> 01:18:29.873
but there's also so many beautiful parts

01:18:29.873 --> 01:18:32.883
and the moments of suffering make the beautiful moment.

01:18:32.883 --> 01:18:34.913
You can't have one without the other.

01:18:34.913 --> 01:18:35.983
That's exactly right.

01:18:35.983 --> 01:18:38.923
You know, you can't and even with that,

01:18:38.923 --> 01:18:41.913
like when you were talking about reaching number one

01:18:41.913 --> 01:18:46.073
and you think, oh, I've been vying for this for a decade

01:18:46.073 --> 01:18:49.683
and then it gets there and it's you find it lacking

01:18:49.683 --> 01:18:52.023
in a satisfaction that you were expecting.

01:18:52.023 --> 01:18:53.833
And that's always the case.

01:18:53.833 --> 01:18:57.163
Because the value is in the endeavor,

01:18:57.163 --> 01:18:59.893
it's in the getting there, it's in the journey undertaken,

01:18:59.893 --> 01:19:02.953
it's in the hardship and the struggle and the suffering,

01:19:02.953 --> 01:19:06.382
the lightened shaft that is packed into

01:19:06.382 --> 01:19:10.343
trying to aspiring to that place.

01:19:10.343 --> 01:19:13.933
It's not the arrival. The arrival is a footnote.

01:19:13.933 --> 01:19:17.283
The journey is over kind of when you write and--

01:19:17.283 --> 01:19:19.251
And then you're there you're like now what?

01:19:19.251 --> 01:19:20.723
Right?
Yeah.

01:19:20.723 --> 01:19:22.663
Like that's a scary place to be.

01:19:22.663 --> 01:19:25.734
Yeah, short of you figuring this out for yourself,

01:19:25.734 --> 01:19:28.933
like short of COVID kind of compelling.

01:19:28.933 --> 01:19:31.133
Now this introspection on your level,

01:19:31.133 --> 01:19:32.423
it would have been interesting to see

01:19:32.423 --> 01:19:33.863
what would have happened.

01:19:33.863 --> 01:19:38.803
It was difficult, like I know COVID has been devastating

01:19:38.803 --> 01:19:41.690
and obviously super painful for a lot of people

01:19:41.690 --> 01:19:45.413
and a lot of loss and it's devastating.

01:19:45.413 --> 01:19:48.193
But in my case it's really,

01:19:48.193 --> 01:19:50.926
I've found a lot of positive in it.

01:19:51.873 --> 01:19:56.733
And before now before I was this chef

01:19:56.733 --> 01:19:59.953
that was named number one at some point,

01:19:59.953 --> 01:20:03.513
and the reservations were booked and booked

01:20:03.513 --> 01:20:04.603
and booked and booked.

01:20:04.603 --> 01:20:09.603
And I became more of a brand than a person.

01:20:10.713 --> 01:20:15.713
And so many of my partners wanted to monetize this brand.

01:20:19.463 --> 01:20:24.463
People saw opportunities to open different concepts,

01:20:25.293 --> 01:20:29.793
you know, casual concept alignment with brands

01:20:29.793 --> 01:20:31.143
and all this kind of stuff.

01:20:32.103 --> 01:20:35.443
And I started completely losing myself.

01:20:35.443 --> 01:20:40.443
I wasn't even in charge of my own schedule in a way anymore.

01:20:40.833 --> 01:20:45.293
It was just like, I was this face of this brand

01:20:45.293 --> 01:20:50.293
that a lot of people were interested in but I wasn't.

01:20:51.543 --> 01:20:53.873
And so it was really challenging.

01:20:53.873 --> 01:20:57.356
And when everything fell apart during COVID,

01:20:58.943 --> 01:21:03.513
I was really able to cut ties with a lot of organizations,

01:21:03.513 --> 01:21:06.343
a lot of partners, a lot of projects

01:21:06.343 --> 01:21:09.693
in a way that I don't know if it would have been possible.

01:21:09.693 --> 01:21:12.863
Yeah, your entrepreneurial journey is fascinating

01:21:12.863 --> 01:21:16.243
as this hybrid of how do you blend art and commerce

01:21:16.243 --> 01:21:18.094
in a way that is successful.

01:21:18.094 --> 01:21:21.143
It's a difficult recipe to get, correct.

01:21:21.143 --> 01:21:23.173
You've had all of these successes along the way

01:21:23.173 --> 01:21:25.943
and all these different kind of incarnations

01:21:25.943 --> 01:21:30.153
of partnerships from Danny Meyer originally bringing you in

01:21:30.153 --> 01:21:32.993
to 11 Madison Park in the first place.

01:21:32.993 --> 01:21:36.072
And then you and Will Gadara,

01:21:36.072 --> 01:21:37.763
is that how you say his last name.

01:21:37.763 --> 01:21:40.703
Kind of purchasing or Danny selling you the restaurant,

01:21:40.703 --> 01:21:42.153
and then you having this partner

01:21:42.153 --> 01:21:44.373
and this fruitful partnership with this guy

01:21:44.373 --> 01:21:48.163
in which you create all of these amazing opportunities,

01:21:48.163 --> 01:21:51.163
but ultimately your life scales out of control.

01:21:51.163 --> 01:21:54.515
You become a brand, you become a licensing vehicle.

01:21:54.515 --> 01:21:58.283
And then to cut ties with all of that

01:21:58.283 --> 01:22:02.433
and narrow your focus to just literally this one,

01:22:02.433 --> 01:22:05.263
like let go of the hotels and all the kind of stuff.

01:22:05.263 --> 01:22:07.028
Just say, I wanna get back to the one thing

01:22:07.028 --> 01:22:10.973
that I love that gives me creative sustenance

01:22:10.973 --> 01:22:13.273
is a bold move in and of itself.

01:22:13.273 --> 01:22:15.953
And now here you are like, you have investors,

01:22:15.953 --> 01:22:17.573
but essentially you're the proprietor

01:22:17.573 --> 01:22:21.283
and sole owner of this restaurant.

01:22:21.283 --> 01:22:22.633
You've got one in London as well,

01:22:22.633 --> 01:22:27.633
but to have the kind of sensibility to say bigger,

01:22:28.443 --> 01:22:29.763
isn't necessarily better.

01:22:29.763 --> 01:22:34.373
It's moving me away from what I'm actually seeking.

01:22:34.373 --> 01:22:39.373
Yeah, you know what's interesting is that all associations

01:22:41.073 --> 01:22:45.610
partnerships in my case, I think or most of all,

01:22:45.610 --> 01:22:49.252
we're actually needed and beneficial.

01:22:49.252 --> 01:22:51.793
And it got me to this point.

01:22:51.793 --> 01:22:55.613
I mean, it's starts with even just in a restaurant.

01:22:55.613 --> 01:22:57.895
When you think about the need for guests,

01:22:57.895 --> 01:23:02.483
in the beginning, you need to kind of cook the food

01:23:02.483 --> 01:23:06.473
that guests will want and will pay for.

01:23:06.473 --> 01:23:10.103
And then only slowly you can kind of come into your own

01:23:10.103 --> 01:23:12.518
and you can add some creativity to it.

01:23:12.518 --> 01:23:13.803
And I tell you what you like.

01:23:13.803 --> 01:23:18.703
Yeah, I mean, I love it.

01:23:18.703 --> 01:23:20.223
I don't know what they want.

01:23:20.223 --> 01:23:21.833
That's my job.

01:23:21.833 --> 01:23:23.983
But we're still doing it today.

01:23:23.983 --> 01:23:26.670
I mean, today we're cooking completely plant-based

01:23:26.670 --> 01:23:28.663
people don't even want, you know,

01:23:28.663 --> 01:23:30.813
know that they would like that.

01:23:30.813 --> 01:23:35.813
But the meal starts with this essence of tomato,

01:23:35.873 --> 01:23:40.873
just simple tea of tomato that is sort of like disarming

01:23:42.073 --> 01:23:44.323
and no matter who you are.

01:23:44.323 --> 01:23:46.363
You're shocking people

01:23:46.363 --> 01:23:48.053
into you're giving them the sense like

01:23:48.053 --> 01:23:50.773
this is gonna be unlike what you expect it to be

01:23:50.773 --> 01:23:53.643
like when it's placed down in front of you.

01:23:53.643 --> 01:23:55.973
There's no explanation of what it is.

01:23:55.973 --> 01:23:57.433
I thought, oh, this is a tea

01:23:57.433 --> 01:23:59.583
or we're gonna cleanse our palette and get ready.

01:23:59.583 --> 01:24:02.293
And then you take one sip and you're like, whoa,

01:24:02.293 --> 01:24:03.953
I've never had anything like that before.

01:24:03.953 --> 01:24:05.543
Certainly you taste the tomato,

01:24:05.543 --> 01:24:08.223
but clearly it's a distillation of,

01:24:08.223 --> 01:24:11.693
I don't know how many thousands of tomatoes are compressed

01:24:11.693 --> 01:24:15.313
to get like that density of flavor into this clear broth.

01:24:15.313 --> 01:24:16.146
Yes.

01:24:16.146 --> 01:24:18.133
And inevitably you think, okay,

01:24:18.133 --> 01:24:20.883
this is gonna be a ride, like no other.

01:24:20.883 --> 01:24:25.883
It's intentionally vague, simple, easy to digest

01:24:26.863 --> 01:24:31.853
and very, very delicious, universal, delicious.

01:24:31.853 --> 01:24:34.983
And so it's, then we can take people on a journey

01:24:34.983 --> 01:24:36.933
then they're like, it's kind of like a hug.

01:24:36.933 --> 01:24:38.813
It's like, it's gonna be a great meal.

01:24:38.813 --> 01:24:40.199
I trust you.

01:24:40.199 --> 01:24:42.403
Exactly and that's with the cooking

01:24:42.403 --> 01:24:43.963
and that's with partnerships.

01:24:43.963 --> 01:24:48.963
Like I needed certain partners to understand

01:24:49.133 --> 01:24:50.733
how business works in America.

01:24:50.733 --> 01:24:52.723
I had no idea, I came from Switzerland.

01:24:52.723 --> 01:24:54.813
I left school at 14.

01:24:54.813 --> 01:24:58.063
Do you know, and now I'm running an empire

01:24:58.063 --> 01:24:59.433
here in New York city.

01:24:59.433 --> 01:25:01.293
I mean, it really is crazy.

01:25:01.293 --> 01:25:05.860
You know, I needed these steps if you will.

01:25:05.860 --> 01:25:10.860
But if you get to the place and proudly I can say,

01:25:11.593 --> 01:25:14.313
I don't have an investor anymore.

01:25:14.313 --> 01:25:16.486
And it took 44 years old.

01:25:16.486 --> 01:25:18.293
Wow, and you're the sole owner.

01:25:18.293 --> 01:25:22.333
Yeah, I could have this decision to go plant-based

01:25:22.333 --> 01:25:26.263
I don't think, I don't know if there's an investor out there

01:25:26.263 --> 01:25:28.353
who would have said, oh, this is a great idea.

01:25:28.353 --> 01:25:31.746
We just had, COVID now go plant-based.

01:25:32.673 --> 01:25:35.956
You know, I don't know if that investor is out there.

01:25:35.956 --> 01:25:37.423
That's liberating

01:25:37.423 --> 01:25:38.323
It's liberating.

01:25:39.325 --> 01:25:40.473
You know, land, you got landlords and all that,

01:25:40.473 --> 01:25:41.703
but you know.

01:25:41.703 --> 01:25:44.413
No, no.
It's pretty cool.

01:25:44.413 --> 01:25:49.413
What is in your mind, the distinction between good and great

01:25:50.613 --> 01:25:53.833
with food, like there are plenty of good chefs.

01:25:53.833 --> 01:25:56.613
What goes into being a great chef

01:25:56.613 --> 01:26:00.633
for people that aren't steeped in this type of this world,

01:26:00.633 --> 01:26:03.893
this subculture, like how do you think about that?

01:26:03.893 --> 01:26:05.453
That's a big question.

01:26:05.453 --> 01:26:07.586
I think, you know, foremost it's,

01:26:08.473 --> 01:26:11.393
I think cooking is, is a craft.

01:26:11.393 --> 01:26:15.479
And I think there are amazing, amazing restaurants

01:26:15.479 --> 01:26:19.663
where you just go and have an amazing meal

01:26:19.663 --> 01:26:22.803
with amazing source ingredients prepared

01:26:22.803 --> 01:26:24.116
in a beautiful way.

01:26:25.223 --> 01:26:28.513
And then in the rare cases,

01:26:28.513 --> 01:26:32.013
it kind of can transcend that

01:26:32.013 --> 01:26:36.713
and it can actually add something.

01:26:36.713 --> 01:26:39.353
I talked about culture earlier, how fine dining

01:26:39.353 --> 01:26:41.801
is a big part of culture.

01:26:41.801 --> 01:26:44.623
And the language of sorts, like, you know,

01:26:44.623 --> 01:26:48.653
we know that there are a lot of different languages

01:26:48.653 --> 01:26:51.943
that the languages of politics and the languages of art

01:26:51.943 --> 01:26:53.173
and different kinds of arts.

01:26:53.173 --> 01:26:55.893
And I think there's a language of food.

01:26:55.893 --> 01:26:58.003
And I think chefs through history of time,

01:26:58.003 --> 01:27:01.646
have, you know, when you think of Alice Waters,

01:27:03.313 --> 01:27:06.643
I don't know it's in the 70's probably when she,

01:27:06.643 --> 01:27:11.643
you know, really made a big stand on local food that was

01:27:12.153 --> 01:27:16.763
ground breaking and it was, it really changed culture.

01:27:16.763 --> 01:27:18.586
It changed America.

01:27:19.433 --> 01:27:22.593
And there's been other chefs in history of time before.

01:27:22.593 --> 01:27:25.803
So I think way rarely there's someone that comes along

01:27:25.803 --> 01:27:30.353
and that really can make that kind of right kind of impact.

01:27:30.353 --> 01:27:33.953
Is it an intangible like I know it when I taste it

01:27:33.953 --> 01:27:37.123
or see it or feel it, or are there criteria

01:27:37.123 --> 01:27:39.643
like when you sit down at a restaurant and you're surprised,

01:27:39.643 --> 01:27:41.853
and you're like, this is great.

01:27:41.853 --> 01:27:46.053
And this is great because XYZ,

01:27:46.053 --> 01:27:50.103
like can it be distilled down into variables

01:27:50.103 --> 01:27:52.903
that you feel are important ingredients

01:27:52.903 --> 01:27:56.303
in making a proclamation that a certain chef

01:27:56.303 --> 01:27:58.263
or a certain restaurant, you know,

01:27:58.263 --> 01:28:01.723
stands or sits above its competitors?

01:28:01.723 --> 01:28:05.403
To me, it's like, when it's bigger than the restaurant,

01:28:05.403 --> 01:28:08.043
to me, it's, there is the craft

01:28:08.043 --> 01:28:11.583
and when you're in a restaurant of great craftsmans

01:28:11.583 --> 01:28:14.530
and great chefs, then you're in the restaurant

01:28:14.530 --> 01:28:18.863
and you taste it and you see the attention to detail

01:28:18.863 --> 01:28:21.773
and the way the flavors are balanced

01:28:21.773 --> 01:28:23.993
and it's harmonious and all that.

01:28:23.993 --> 01:28:26.508
And then there are a few restaurants

01:28:26.508 --> 01:28:30.103
where it's bigger than the restaurant

01:28:30.103 --> 01:28:33.303
where it's about something bigger and you,

01:28:33.303 --> 01:28:34.883
and that's just a feeling.

01:28:34.883 --> 01:28:37.113
And that's just when it's like,

01:28:37.113 --> 01:28:39.713
almost like it gets some construct

01:28:39.713 --> 01:28:44.713
where everything is from the place to the people

01:28:46.503 --> 01:28:50.363
to dedication of the people, to the plate where,

01:28:50.363 --> 01:28:54.173
to where it's this one idea of, I guess, I'm construct.

01:28:54.173 --> 01:28:59.173
And then it can really make real big impact

01:28:59.373 --> 01:29:01.903
that will trickle down to all kinds of industries.

01:29:01.903 --> 01:29:03.943
Yeah, there's the, you know, hero dreams

01:29:03.943 --> 01:29:06.613
of sushi type mastering.

01:29:06.613 --> 01:29:08.903
And then there's the kind of orchestration

01:29:08.903 --> 01:29:12.523
of scaling up that mastery to cultivate

01:29:12.523 --> 01:29:15.022
an extraordinary experience.

01:29:15.022 --> 01:29:16.646
It's interesting.

01:29:17.533 --> 01:29:20.783
It's something that I think, you know,

01:29:20.783 --> 01:29:23.843
to kind of pivot back to the mystical a little bit,

01:29:23.843 --> 01:29:25.183
there's a saying,

01:29:25.183 --> 01:29:27.843
I was talking to Jeff about this last night,

01:29:27.843 --> 01:29:28.913
long time in recovery,

01:29:28.913 --> 01:29:31.415
and there's an adage in alcoholics anonymous

01:29:31.415 --> 01:29:33.883
that you can't transmit something you haven't got,

01:29:33.883 --> 01:29:36.503
like you have to carry the vibration within you

01:29:36.503 --> 01:29:39.933
to be the lighthouse that will attract the energy

01:29:39.933 --> 01:29:43.337
into your life that you aspire to have.

01:29:43.337 --> 01:29:44.883
You can't fake that.

01:29:44.883 --> 01:29:47.633
Like you're either living it and you're being it,

01:29:47.633 --> 01:29:49.033
or you're a pretender.

01:29:49.033 --> 01:29:54.033
And increasingly I think people are antenna for what is real

01:29:54.053 --> 01:29:57.403
and what is authentic is never been more finely honed.

01:29:57.403 --> 01:30:00.263
Like we can all see coming a million miles away,

01:30:00.263 --> 01:30:04.513
and we can all identify when something is real

01:30:04.513 --> 01:30:08.113
or something is different or carrying that vibration

01:30:08.113 --> 01:30:09.933
that we wanna be around.

01:30:09.933 --> 01:30:12.643
You know, and I think the more that you instill that

01:30:12.643 --> 01:30:14.813
in yourself and in the culture that you create

01:30:14.813 --> 01:30:16.383
in your kitchen, you know,

01:30:16.383 --> 01:30:18.843
the more that, that will resonate out into the world

01:30:18.843 --> 01:30:21.673
and attract those kinds of people into this mission

01:30:21.673 --> 01:30:25.193
that you're on of transforming food and security.

01:30:25.193 --> 01:30:27.363
I think that's really well said.

01:30:27.363 --> 01:30:29.413
And I think so true.

01:30:29.413 --> 01:30:34.276
I think authenticity, you feel if it's authentic or not.

01:30:34.276 --> 01:30:38.423
And I think the one thing I do believe though,

01:30:38.423 --> 01:30:43.103
is that if you do not perfect the craft,

01:30:43.103 --> 01:30:44.552
you will never get.

01:30:44.552 --> 01:30:46.000
It's lost right to the neck.

01:30:46.000 --> 01:30:49.243
And you can't, there's a lot of people who break rules,

01:30:49.243 --> 01:30:52.513
but they they never took the time to master the craft itself

01:30:52.513 --> 01:30:53.823
and really understand the rules.

01:30:53.823 --> 01:30:55.883
Like you can't paint outside the lines

01:30:55.883 --> 01:30:57.943
until you really know how to paint

01:30:57.943 --> 01:31:00.003
you know, with mastery within the lines.

01:31:00.003 --> 01:31:05.003
Yeah, that's why art is such a great inspiration to me.

01:31:05.783 --> 01:31:10.543
And I, you know, most of my friends are painters

01:31:10.543 --> 01:31:13.143
or sculptors or dancers.

01:31:13.143 --> 01:31:18.143
And it is so interesting when you follow,

01:31:19.673 --> 01:31:23.403
for example, Mondrian, most people know, you know,

01:31:23.403 --> 01:31:26.954
Mondrian one of the first abstract painters.

01:31:26.954 --> 01:31:30.553
But when you look at his early paintings,

01:31:30.553 --> 01:31:31.833
they weren't abstract at all.

01:31:31.833 --> 01:31:36.493
There are landscapes and beautiful ones,

01:31:36.493 --> 01:31:39.993
but then at some point he became Mondrian.

01:31:39.993 --> 01:31:42.133
It's always amazing to see early works

01:31:42.133 --> 01:31:44.743
of some of those more modern artists

01:31:44.743 --> 01:31:47.763
who are doing radically different things

01:31:47.763 --> 01:31:49.683
and to realize like, oh, they actually,

01:31:49.683 --> 01:31:51.153
they can do it the other way.

01:31:51.153 --> 01:31:53.166
Yeah, that's right.

01:31:53.166 --> 01:31:54.003
That's makes it brilliant.

01:31:54.003 --> 01:31:55.153
Yeah.
Right.

01:31:55.153 --> 01:31:59.853
Well, art has been so crucial and influential

01:31:59.853 --> 01:32:01.533
in how you think about food

01:32:01.533 --> 01:32:03.483
and just the restaurant experience in general.

01:32:03.483 --> 01:32:06.023
So talk a little bit about that influence

01:32:06.023 --> 01:32:09.553
and also how it's kind of informed some of these pivots,

01:32:09.553 --> 01:32:13.926
you know, specifically going from this very,

01:32:15.393 --> 01:32:17.181
I wouldn't say sensory overload experience,

01:32:17.181 --> 01:32:20.703
but kind of a robust sensory experience

01:32:20.703 --> 01:32:25.703
to trimming things down into a more minimalistic approach.

01:32:25.783 --> 01:32:27.133
I've heard you talk about minimalism

01:32:27.133 --> 01:32:30.373
before I would characterize it more as essentialism,

01:32:30.373 --> 01:32:34.433
like getting rid of anything that's not informing,

01:32:34.433 --> 01:32:36.153
you know, the experience or the art

01:32:36.153 --> 01:32:37.783
that you're trying to share.

01:32:37.783 --> 01:32:40.723
Yeah, I think that's really good observation.

01:32:40.723 --> 01:32:43.693
I actually call it elemental,

01:32:43.693 --> 01:32:46.403
like where everything is stared that's needed,

01:32:46.403 --> 01:32:47.615
but nothing.

01:32:47.615 --> 01:32:49.754
Nothing extraneous.
Yes.

01:32:49.754 --> 01:32:53.443
For me, when I was 12 years old,

01:32:53.443 --> 01:32:56.293
I had my first real experience with art

01:32:56.293 --> 01:32:59.673
and my parents took me to the Lorawan Sheree in Paris.

01:32:59.673 --> 01:33:03.763
And, you know, that's where it's the museum

01:33:03.763 --> 01:33:08.323
where they hold the large Monet Water Lily paintings.

01:33:08.323 --> 01:33:10.443
And it's sort of these two oval rooms

01:33:10.443 --> 01:33:12.993
of just Monet Waterlilies.

01:33:12.993 --> 01:33:16.653
And as a 12 year old kid, you just feel like

01:33:17.749 --> 01:33:20.133
you're totally wrapped into these paintings.

01:33:20.133 --> 01:33:23.483
And for the first time I had this experience,

01:33:23.483 --> 01:33:25.883
when I didn't know how I was feeling,

01:33:25.883 --> 01:33:27.223
did I, was I happy?

01:33:27.223 --> 01:33:28.153
Was I sad?

01:33:28.153 --> 01:33:29.183
It was really bizarre.

01:33:29.183 --> 01:33:31.573
It was very emotional.

01:33:31.573 --> 01:33:36.473
And since then I knew that art was speaking to me

01:33:36.473 --> 01:33:39.783
and was really important to me.

01:33:39.783 --> 01:33:44.783
And I think forever since then, I've put wherever I go,

01:33:45.533 --> 01:33:47.493
I visit artists studios.

01:33:47.493 --> 01:33:52.493
I go to museums, I go to galleries and I read about art.

01:33:53.133 --> 01:33:56.903
And I think I had another really meaningful experience.

01:33:56.903 --> 01:33:59.993
And that was probably at the age of like 24

01:33:59.993 --> 01:34:03.926
when I saw for the first time I saw Lucho Fontana,

01:34:04.923 --> 01:34:09.873
these paintings where he it's just a canvas, a white canvas,

01:34:09.873 --> 01:34:12.998
and he took a knife and he just cuts through the canvas.

01:34:12.998 --> 01:34:13.831
Yeah.

01:34:13.831 --> 01:34:15.993
And a friend of mine took me to see that show

01:34:15.993 --> 01:34:19.020
and said, "Hey, this is a really important artist.

01:34:19.020 --> 01:34:23.843
"And you should, you know, really dig in on him."

01:34:23.843 --> 01:34:25.463
And I didn't really understand what--

01:34:25.463 --> 01:34:26.953
What the fuck the is this.
Exactly.

01:34:26.953 --> 01:34:28.223
He literally took a knife

01:34:28.223 --> 01:34:30.393
and slashed the middle of a white canvas.

01:34:30.393 --> 01:34:33.263
But my friend was very knowledgeable so I trusted him.

01:34:33.263 --> 01:34:35.683
And in the next few weeks,

01:34:35.683 --> 01:34:39.773
I tried to understand why this painting was so important.

01:34:39.773 --> 01:34:42.543
And it's exactly to the point that you said,

01:34:42.543 --> 01:34:46.803
because he was Fontana was while a great sculptor.

01:34:46.803 --> 01:34:48.343
He worked in ceremony Airmax,

01:34:48.343 --> 01:34:50.793
but he could also paint anything.

01:34:50.793 --> 01:34:53.223
I mean, he was just a really accomplished artist.

01:34:53.223 --> 01:34:55.303
And at some point he decided that

01:34:55.303 --> 01:34:59.613
he would just cut the canvas and that's his master piece.

01:34:59.613 --> 01:35:03.083
And that painting in fact changed art forever.

01:35:03.083 --> 01:35:07.193
Because with that one slid in the canvas,

01:35:07.193 --> 01:35:12.193
he questioned everything that sort of was there before,

01:35:12.263 --> 01:35:15.793
like does the canvas need to have a painting?

01:35:15.793 --> 01:35:17.673
Is art on the canvas?

01:35:17.673 --> 01:35:19.793
Could the canvas be the art?

01:35:19.793 --> 01:35:22.873
Could the artwork be behind the canvas?

01:35:22.873 --> 01:35:25.743
I mean, he questions, everything is painting sculpture

01:35:25.743 --> 01:35:29.923
Or is the art the conversation around the work itself.

01:35:29.923 --> 01:35:31.993
Yeah, as well
Yeah.

01:35:31.993 --> 01:35:33.173
So taking that like,

01:35:33.173 --> 01:35:35.393
what do you make of that for yourself?

01:35:35.393 --> 01:35:37.503
Like why was that so impactful to you

01:35:37.503 --> 01:35:38.793
and how does that get channeled

01:35:38.793 --> 01:35:40.853
into how you think about food

01:35:40.853 --> 01:35:44.213
and the experiences that you're providing?

01:35:44.213 --> 01:35:46.693
The more I thought about that work was

01:35:47.843 --> 01:35:52.843
he changed art forever with the way minimal gesture.

01:35:53.343 --> 01:35:58.343
And for me, that was just so powerful and so mind blowing.

01:35:58.493 --> 01:36:02.273
And so this is how I wanted to cook.

01:36:02.273 --> 01:36:06.516
I wanted to cook very minimal, but say a lot.

01:36:07.563 --> 01:36:12.443
And I realized that was a lot harder than,

01:36:12.443 --> 01:36:17.443
than it seemed because the gesture has to be so strong

01:36:17.653 --> 01:36:21.493
and there is nowhere to hide.

01:36:21.493 --> 01:36:25.383
And as a young chef, often I had two ingredients

01:36:25.383 --> 01:36:30.363
on the plate and I was like, that's not a complete dish.

01:36:30.363 --> 01:36:33.123
No one would think that's great.

01:36:33.123 --> 01:36:34.773
So then I added things.

01:36:34.773 --> 01:36:37.723
I said, oh, I need to know the puree and other ingredients

01:36:37.723 --> 01:36:39.713
and other texture.

01:36:39.713 --> 01:36:42.023
And it became more complicated.

01:36:42.023 --> 01:36:45.713
But at that point, you know, guests were reacting

01:36:45.713 --> 01:36:48.203
to this more than they would have

01:36:48.203 --> 01:36:50.093
with two ingredients on the plate.

01:36:50.093 --> 01:36:54.003
And it took a long time for me to get to the place

01:36:54.003 --> 01:36:56.373
where I could have two ingredients on the plate.

01:36:56.373 --> 01:37:01.313
And it probably only happened like four years ago.

01:37:01.313 --> 01:37:03.443
In the way that was it, Alice Waters,

01:37:03.443 --> 01:37:07.433
who would just say, here's two dates on a plate that's it.

01:37:07.433 --> 01:37:09.363
It takes a lot to get there.

01:37:09.363 --> 01:37:11.803
It takes a certain level of audacity.

01:37:11.803 --> 01:37:13.543
Confidence.
Yeah.

01:37:13.543 --> 01:37:15.843
But you're not lacking in confidence.

01:37:15.843 --> 01:37:16.900
Well, thank you.

01:37:18.175 --> 01:37:20.356
I don't know if that's a compliment, you know,

01:37:21.254 --> 01:37:24.713
no, I mean, it's, I guess,

01:37:24.713 --> 01:37:28.643
you know, courage is probably a better word, like to say.

01:37:28.643 --> 01:37:32.463
I have a strong enough sense of self in what I'm doing,

01:37:32.463 --> 01:37:34.503
that I can put this out there

01:37:34.503 --> 01:37:37.603
and maybe not be certain, but have some level of confidence

01:37:37.603 --> 01:37:40.333
that it will be received because it's backed up

01:37:40.333 --> 01:37:41.860
by this lifetime of experience.

01:37:41.860 --> 01:37:45.579
And this trust that you've encountered gendered over time.

01:37:45.579 --> 01:37:46.412
Is that accurate?

01:37:46.412 --> 01:37:48.245
How does that one feel?

01:37:48.245 --> 01:37:50.912
(both laughing)

01:37:52.363 --> 01:37:54.696
I backed myself in a corner.

01:37:55.773 --> 01:37:56.613
You know, what am saying.

01:37:56.613 --> 01:38:01.256
It takes a long time to be very simple.

01:38:03.583 --> 01:38:04.416
Yeah
Yeah.

01:38:04.416 --> 01:38:08.673
Yeah, and that's reflected in the, in the Rita Ackerman

01:38:08.673 --> 01:38:09.903
painting in the restaurant.

01:38:09.903 --> 01:38:13.453
The chalkboard painting that's very stripped down.

01:38:13.453 --> 01:38:16.433
That's on one far side of the wall hanging,

01:38:16.433 --> 01:38:20.533
hanging high up that kind of to me symbolizes,

01:38:20.533 --> 01:38:24.923
like we can always erase this and start fresh or new,

01:38:24.923 --> 01:38:26.313
like nothing is permanent.

01:38:26.313 --> 01:38:29.813
It's a reminder, like this is a process.

01:38:29.813 --> 01:38:33.503
Yeah, it's like Picasso said,

01:38:33.503 --> 01:38:38.380
you know, "A painting is it's never finished

01:38:39.250 --> 01:38:40.963
"it's only abandoned."

01:38:40.963 --> 01:38:42.503
Yeah, and I think that's true.

01:38:42.503 --> 01:38:44.413
That's true of everything that's true

01:38:44.413 --> 01:38:48.323
Whether you're writing a book or preparing meals,

01:38:48.323 --> 01:38:51.013
I mean, the difference with cuisine,

01:38:51.013 --> 01:38:53.533
yes, there's this constant every day you're doing it.

01:38:53.533 --> 01:38:56.683
It's evolving, it's an organic living breathing thing.

01:38:56.683 --> 01:39:00.803
But each meal in and of itself is almost like a Zen Mandola

01:39:00.803 --> 01:39:04.043
because it will be consumed and destroyed, yeah.

01:39:04.043 --> 01:39:05.434
What's hard in our.

01:39:05.434 --> 01:39:06.466
It's impermanent.

01:39:06.466 --> 01:39:08.336
Yeah it is impermanent and, you know,

01:39:08.336 --> 01:39:10.963
there's an artist called Andy Goltz

01:39:10.963 --> 01:39:13.743
where the, which we're very inspired by

01:39:13.743 --> 01:39:15.783
and he works in nature a lot

01:39:15.783 --> 01:39:20.783
And and he makes like sculptures out of icicles and,

01:39:21.373 --> 01:39:23.860
he builds them and then the sun come.

01:39:23.860 --> 01:39:24.803
And they're gone.

01:39:24.803 --> 01:39:27.753
And they're gone or he ties these leaves together

01:39:27.753 --> 01:39:30.743
and he lets them run down the river and it's so beautiful,

01:39:30.743 --> 01:39:32.126
but it's impermanent.

01:39:33.743 --> 01:39:35.533
It's hard to...

01:39:35.533 --> 01:39:39.563
It's beautiful because I think a lot in life,

01:39:39.563 --> 01:39:43.265
the impermanent things make it magical.

01:39:43.265 --> 01:39:47.673
But as a creator, it's also difficult

01:39:47.673 --> 01:39:51.303
because you know, you had dinner there last night

01:39:51.303 --> 01:39:54.943
and there was someone else gonna have dinner tonight.

01:39:54.943 --> 01:39:59.503
And we have to recreate that moment over and over

01:39:59.503 --> 01:40:00.751
and over again.

01:40:00.751 --> 01:40:04.883
Do you think about the kind of mystical aspect

01:40:04.883 --> 01:40:07.967
like putting things inside of people's bodies.

01:40:07.967 --> 01:40:09.803
Like I've created this thing

01:40:09.803 --> 01:40:13.623
it carries a certain vibration and a certain vitality.

01:40:13.623 --> 01:40:15.143
Other people are consuming that.

01:40:15.143 --> 01:40:17.203
And then they're going out into the world,

01:40:17.203 --> 01:40:19.433
this sort of diaspora of human beings

01:40:19.433 --> 01:40:21.233
who are carrying that vibration.

01:40:21.233 --> 01:40:22.993
I mean, Jeff was talking about last night

01:40:22.993 --> 01:40:24.963
the first time he ate at the restaurant

01:40:24.963 --> 01:40:28.193
and you know, as a long-time food critic can writer

01:40:28.193 --> 01:40:29.633
he is used to go these restaurants

01:40:29.633 --> 01:40:31.613
and they kind of just give him a million dishes

01:40:31.613 --> 01:40:33.673
and he rolls out of there and he feels like shit.

01:40:33.673 --> 01:40:35.823
You know, even though the food is boring,

01:40:35.823 --> 01:40:39.263
but in the case of 11 Madison Park with the new menu

01:40:39.263 --> 01:40:42.613
feeling like vital and elevated

01:40:42.613 --> 01:40:45.963
he said like a couple of days, you know,

01:40:45.963 --> 01:40:49.163
even though he's not like he wouldn't consider himself,

01:40:49.163 --> 01:40:50.643
plant-based, I think he's, you know,

01:40:50.643 --> 01:40:52.363
playing around with that in his idea.

01:40:52.363 --> 01:40:54.279
But there is something to that of

01:40:54.279 --> 01:40:57.903
you are providing a certain wavelength of energy

01:40:57.903 --> 01:40:59.823
and putting it into other people's bodies.

01:40:59.823 --> 01:41:02.803
And that has like that activates people

01:41:02.803 --> 01:41:06.203
and puts them on their own kind of journey of exploration,

01:41:06.203 --> 01:41:10.063
their own like trajectory of like learning and exploring

01:41:10.063 --> 01:41:12.113
and growing and evolving.

01:41:12.113 --> 01:41:16.623
Yeah, I mean, it's a humbling to know that,

01:41:16.623 --> 01:41:21.623
you know, what we're creating people are eating ingestion.

01:41:22.533 --> 01:41:26.063
And I think there's, it's really interesting,

01:41:26.063 --> 01:41:30.816
even beyond our knowledge of what's actually happening

01:41:30.816 --> 01:41:32.543
during this meal.

01:41:32.543 --> 01:41:34.373
Like we...

01:41:34.373 --> 01:41:36.533
It's a fully plant-based meal,

01:41:36.533 --> 01:41:40.360
but we're working a lot more with different cultures.

01:41:40.360 --> 01:41:41.903
And that actually, you know,

01:41:41.903 --> 01:41:46.903
when we talk about removing a lot of ingredients of animals,

01:41:47.663 --> 01:41:49.983
the one ingredient that came up a lot

01:41:49.983 --> 01:41:52.223
was the ingredient of time.

01:41:52.223 --> 01:41:56.283
Like the time it takes to ferment something,

01:41:56.283 --> 01:41:58.853
or you know, we're working on all kinds

01:41:58.853 --> 01:42:03.813
of different fermentations of sauces and show use.

01:42:03.813 --> 01:42:07.613
And you get an like a soy sauce is like, gives you an idea,

01:42:07.613 --> 01:42:10.183
but there are so many.

01:42:10.183 --> 01:42:11.016
It's endless.

01:42:11.016 --> 01:42:14.443
It's endless what you can do, not from soy,

01:42:14.443 --> 01:42:16.703
but from so many other things.

01:42:16.703 --> 01:42:20.413
And when we removed meat

01:42:20.413 --> 01:42:25.413
we felt like we wanted to still have a meal

01:42:27.373 --> 01:42:30.613
that is balanced of like where there are some dishes

01:42:30.613 --> 01:42:33.823
are more light, some dishes are more,

01:42:33.823 --> 01:42:37.363
I'm gonna use the word meaty, but richer.

01:42:37.363 --> 01:42:42.273
And so Umami is definitely very central in this cooking.

01:42:42.273 --> 01:42:44.333
We also worked with these Buddhist monks

01:42:44.333 --> 01:42:48.413
who are cooking a cuisine called Shogun.

01:42:48.413 --> 01:42:51.703
And we learned about the ingredient of time

01:42:51.703 --> 01:42:54.303
we learned about using different cultures

01:42:54.303 --> 01:42:59.303
in different dishes, like cultures from, yeah,

01:43:01.673 --> 01:43:04.423
different, you know, we ha we were working with a lab

01:43:04.423 --> 01:43:05.883
called kingdom culture,

01:43:05.883 --> 01:43:10.033
who they have a library of cultures from around the world

01:43:10.033 --> 01:43:12.923
from like Sauerkraut or kimchi or.

01:43:12.923 --> 01:43:15.913
Cultures you didn't, you mean not populations of people,

01:43:15.913 --> 01:43:19.593
you mean literal like biological cultures.

01:43:19.593 --> 01:43:20.426
Lamentation.

01:43:20.426 --> 01:43:21.259
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:43:21.259 --> 01:43:25.073
And I think it affects people.

01:43:25.073 --> 01:43:26.393
I think during a meal,

01:43:26.393 --> 01:43:30.436
having eating these biological cultures

01:43:30.436 --> 01:43:33.703
is affecting people almost to a place where

01:43:33.703 --> 01:43:36.703
people feel euphoric in a beautiful way.

01:43:36.703 --> 01:43:39.303
And I think that's what Jeff is sort of talking about.

01:43:39.303 --> 01:43:41.493
And we don't even fully understand.

01:43:41.493 --> 01:43:43.451
I think Michael Pollan said that,

01:43:43.451 --> 01:43:47.333
you know, we know the body so well,

01:43:47.333 --> 01:43:49.413
many parts of the body we know so well,

01:43:49.413 --> 01:43:52.633
but actually what's happening in our gut.

01:43:52.633 --> 01:43:55.763
Our knowledge is so limited.

01:43:55.763 --> 01:43:59.783
It's like, so I think there there's a lot of work

01:43:59.783 --> 01:44:01.853
that's still that's gonna happen.

01:44:01.853 --> 01:44:04.333
Yeah, it is this amazing amalgam

01:44:04.333 --> 01:44:06.793
of all these different influences and cultures.

01:44:06.793 --> 01:44:11.393
You mentioned the Japanese tradition,

01:44:11.393 --> 01:44:14.733
Buddhist temple food, ideas from India, China,

01:44:14.733 --> 01:44:17.693
Nouvelle cuisine from France in the 70s,

01:44:17.693 --> 01:44:22.603
Boulay, Patterson, Pissarro, like all of these influences

01:44:22.603 --> 01:44:24.143
being kind of hybridized

01:44:24.143 --> 01:44:29.143
and uniquely kind of through your, you know,

01:44:30.003 --> 01:44:32.526
unique perspective creating something new all of that.

01:44:32.526 --> 01:44:37.526
The host last night was sharing about the grinding

01:44:39.433 --> 01:44:42.880
of the Sesame seeds every day

01:44:42.880 --> 01:44:46.163
and how that's almost become, it's like a team activity.

01:44:46.163 --> 01:44:49.223
But it's almost like a group meditation

01:44:49.223 --> 01:44:52.993
that begins every day with this laborious process.

01:44:52.993 --> 01:44:54.234
That's amazing.

01:44:54.234 --> 01:44:58.273
Yeah, and like, how about like the people

01:44:58.273 --> 01:45:01.686
that have to chop the cucumber in the daikon?

01:45:03.723 --> 01:45:05.893
One dish, where you spend all day just doing that

01:45:05.893 --> 01:45:07.723
for that one dish.

01:45:07.723 --> 01:45:11.463
Yeah, it's extremely labor intensive.

01:45:11.463 --> 01:45:13.603
But there's something about that's beautiful.

01:45:13.603 --> 01:45:17.223
It's like so much heart is required to do that

01:45:17.223 --> 01:45:19.683
when you take it in, there is some,

01:45:19.683 --> 01:45:23.133
I think there is something intangible about like that,

01:45:23.133 --> 01:45:24.953
that is impactful.

01:45:24.953 --> 01:45:29.953
One of the Buddhist monks who was with us

01:45:30.063 --> 01:45:33.623
for this last year in New York, his name is Toshio.

01:45:33.623 --> 01:45:37.743
And he said, he thinks that we should have the guests

01:45:37.743 --> 01:45:41.183
also (indistinct) before their meal.

01:45:41.183 --> 01:45:43.933
We should yes, yes.

01:45:43.933 --> 01:45:46.253
Here, this is gonna cost you an arm and a leg

01:45:46.253 --> 01:45:47.613
now go in the back room.

01:45:47.613 --> 01:45:49.276
Cause you can't appreciate it.

01:45:50.141 --> 01:45:51.873
How could you possibly truly appreciate

01:45:51.873 --> 01:45:53.203
what has gone into this

01:45:53.203 --> 01:45:55.820
unless you're actually doing some part of it.

01:45:55.820 --> 01:46:00.573
But you know, I mean, it's a wild idea,

01:46:00.573 --> 01:46:05.063
but when you do the Sesame grounding

01:46:05.063 --> 01:46:10.063
is central to the cooking, descend, Buddhist cooking.

01:46:10.613 --> 01:46:15.613
And you literally sit on a floor on your knee for an hour

01:46:16.783 --> 01:46:18.693
and you're with this wooden pedal

01:46:18.693 --> 01:46:22.873
and you're grinding the Sesame seeds counterclockwise

01:46:22.873 --> 01:46:24.983
because on this side of the earth,

01:46:24.983 --> 01:46:28.213
everything in nature goes counter clockwise.

01:46:28.213 --> 01:46:32.063
And first of all, it's really painful to sit for an hour

01:46:32.063 --> 01:46:32.953
on your knees.

01:46:32.953 --> 01:46:34.973
Like almost unbearable.

01:46:34.973 --> 01:46:37.183
It's unbearable the first few times you do it.

01:46:37.183 --> 01:46:39.303
And then it becomes sort of bearable.

01:46:39.303 --> 01:46:42.641
And then to grind for an hour by hand is hard.

01:46:42.641 --> 01:46:47.641
And it's this meditation, that's ought it's like passion.

01:46:49.342 --> 01:46:54.342
You know, you'll really have to work for it.

01:46:54.423 --> 01:46:58.983
But then when you're done, you're in a different mindset.

01:46:58.983 --> 01:47:00.323
And I think that's his point

01:47:00.323 --> 01:47:02.343
of having the guests participate.

01:47:02.343 --> 01:47:04.913
That's the power it cleanses at.

01:47:04.913 --> 01:47:08.073
That's the required preparation to put you in the state

01:47:08.073 --> 01:47:09.423
of mind to receive.

01:47:09.423 --> 01:47:11.343
That's right, appropriately.

01:47:11.343 --> 01:47:13.063
Wow, we gotta wrap this up

01:47:13.063 --> 01:47:14.813
cause I know you've gotta go in a minute here,

01:47:14.813 --> 01:47:18.183
but I think a good place to kind of close it

01:47:18.183 --> 01:47:20.813
is to talk a little bit about the New York

01:47:20.813 --> 01:47:25.813
of your imagination like if you can continue to scale,

01:47:26.033 --> 01:47:30.763
rethink and you start getting more invested

01:47:30.763 --> 01:47:34.363
in the communities that require food assistance,

01:47:34.363 --> 01:47:37.283
like how can this transform this city

01:47:37.283 --> 01:47:41.903
and set an example for the rest of the country in the world,

01:47:41.903 --> 01:47:45.643
because basically what happens in New York,

01:47:45.643 --> 01:47:47.773
like then percolates out like New York

01:47:47.773 --> 01:47:52.073
is the place from which culture emanates in so many ways.

01:47:52.073 --> 01:47:53.433
And you have this opportunity

01:47:53.433 --> 01:47:56.583
that you're taking advantage of to lead by example.

01:47:56.583 --> 01:47:58.513
So how does this, you know,

01:47:58.513 --> 01:48:01.713
how would you like the world to perceive this and how can

01:48:01.713 --> 01:48:03.823
people or restaurant tours

01:48:03.823 --> 01:48:07.343
or chefs or cooks who are tuning in for this

01:48:07.343 --> 01:48:10.183
start being part of this solution as well?

01:48:10.183 --> 01:48:13.546
Well, I think the one thing to remember is that,

01:48:14.703 --> 01:48:17.933
sometimes you get,

01:48:17.933 --> 01:48:22.003
when you hear the numbers of food insecure people

01:48:22.003 --> 01:48:23.933
in New York or America,

01:48:23.933 --> 01:48:27.686
the number is so big that you ask them to feel like, wow,

01:48:28.532 --> 01:48:31.193
I'm not able to make a difference here.

01:48:31.193 --> 01:48:32.743
What differences that make

01:48:32.743 --> 01:48:36.323
like the number of food insecure, New Yorkers

01:48:36.323 --> 01:48:38.723
during the pandemic was 2 million,

01:48:38.723 --> 01:48:40.383
2 million people a day.

01:48:40.383 --> 01:48:45.383
And so we cooked 5,000 meals a day, which is a lot of meals.

01:48:46.443 --> 01:48:49.463
Just imagine the amount of boxes, trucks, everything,

01:48:49.463 --> 01:48:53.263
5,000 compared to a million it's nothing.

01:48:53.263 --> 01:48:57.163
So on some days I was like, wow,

01:48:57.163 --> 01:49:00.093
we're never gonna make a dent into this.

01:49:00.093 --> 01:49:03.213
But then when you go into these neighborhoods

01:49:03.213 --> 01:49:06.453
and you're actually giving the meals away,

01:49:06.453 --> 01:49:10.683
you're realizing that every single drop matters

01:49:10.683 --> 01:49:12.353
and every meal matters.

01:49:12.353 --> 01:49:14.133
And so--

01:49:14.133 --> 01:49:16.013
I think it's not a drop to them.

01:49:16.013 --> 01:49:18.123
It's not a drop to them.

01:49:18.123 --> 01:49:21.203
And I think that's when it, if everyone out there

01:49:22.213 --> 01:49:26.553
does what they can, the smallest thing matters.

01:49:26.553 --> 01:49:30.903
And in their community, there are amazing organizations

01:49:30.903 --> 01:49:35.003
all over and they're always looking for help.

01:49:35.003 --> 01:49:38.343
Yeah, I mean, there are plenty of other organizations

01:49:38.343 --> 01:49:41.123
out there that are doing this kind of work.

01:49:41.123 --> 01:49:43.853
I just had Maggie Baird on the podcast.

01:49:43.853 --> 01:49:45.163
Who's Billy Eilishs' mom.

01:49:45.163 --> 01:49:47.123
She's got an organization called support and feed.

01:49:47.123 --> 01:49:49.673
She's doing essentially the same thing.

01:49:49.673 --> 01:49:52.413
Jaden Smith in LA has his, I Love You Truck.

01:49:52.413 --> 01:49:55.662
He goes down to Skid Row and he delivers plant-based meals.

01:49:55.662 --> 01:49:56.495
Amazing.

01:49:56.495 --> 01:49:57.623
But I feel like the power of Rethink

01:49:57.623 --> 01:50:00.053
is to create these organizational tools,

01:50:00.053 --> 01:50:04.003
to establish efficiencies in communication and distribution

01:50:04.003 --> 01:50:07.953
so that all of the various people who are doing good work

01:50:07.953 --> 01:50:10.903
can align in a certain way that can,

01:50:10.903 --> 01:50:13.273
you know, establish the kind of scale required

01:50:13.273 --> 01:50:16.853
to address the problem in a macro meaningful way.

01:50:16.853 --> 01:50:19.953
I think using the infrastructure that already exists

01:50:19.953 --> 01:50:23.823
is probably the most powerful way we can actually

01:50:23.823 --> 01:50:26.763
make a difference and in particular,

01:50:26.763 --> 01:50:31.403
if we can even make the infrastructure stronger,

01:50:31.403 --> 01:50:34.763
because there are already funds a lot of funds

01:50:34.763 --> 01:50:36.923
going towards food insecurity,

01:50:36.923 --> 01:50:40.563
but a lot of the systems are so antiquated,

01:50:40.563 --> 01:50:43.073
Some of these food banks and where the money goes to,

01:50:43.073 --> 01:50:45.553
it's just so antiquated.

01:50:45.553 --> 01:50:50.553
So I think that has to be rethought to where the money flows

01:50:51.723 --> 01:50:54.743
and using the existing infrastructure

01:50:54.743 --> 01:50:55.883
that can strengthen it.

01:50:55.883 --> 01:50:57.643
Yeah, good man.

